o its `refreshAtMs` is a real answer to\n   * \"when can I use this again\" after the quota runs out. The probe passes it\n   * through; a failure to read it is not an error, because a probe without a\n   * cooldown is still a useful probe.\n   */\n  const quotaRefreshOf = async (\n    region: WorkBuddyRegion,\n    credential: Awaited<ReturnType<WorkBuddyCredentialStore['resolve']>>,\n  ): Promise<number | undefined> => {\n    try {\n      const credits = await client.fetchCredits(credential)\n      const refreshes = credits.packages\n        .filter(pack => pack.monthly && pack.refreshAtMs !== undefined)\n        .map(pack => pack.refreshAtMs as number)\n      return refreshes.length === 0 ? undefined : Math.min(...refreshes)\n    } catch {\n      return undefined\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The next account to try for a request whose current account just failed,\n   * or `undefined` when this region's pool has nobody left.\n   *\n   * Reads the pool's EXISTING measurements instead of probing: a request that\n   * just failed must not spend further requests deciding who serves next (and a\n   * probe of the target model would itself be billed). Candidates are this\n   * region's checked-in members in rank order, minus everyone this request has\n   * already tried, minus anyone a measurement says cannot serve right now — a\n   * limited account whose stated cooldown has not elapsed, or one whose\n   * credential the upstream rejected.\n   *\n   * The pool switch gates this: off means the user's account serves every\n   * request and a failure is reported as-is, exactly as before the pool existed.\n   *\n   * Reads the candidate through `credentialFor()`, never `resolve()`: the saved\n   * selection and the store's runtime state stay untouched, so this is\n   * per-request borrowing rather than a silent change of who pays.\n   *\n   * Uses `localPoolMembers`, NOT `poolMembersOf`. This runs on the failure path\n   * of a request the user is already waiting on, and `poolMembersOf` fetches\n   * credits per member — so the expensive version turned one failed request into\n   * N extra upstream calls before the retry even left. The doc above always said\n   * \"must not spend further requests\"; the code used to contradict it. Credits\n   * still steer the ranking when a recent snapshot exists, because\n   * `localPoolMembers` reads the one the card's poll already paid for.\n   */\n  const failoverAccountFor = async (\n    region: WorkBuddyRegion,\n    triedAccountIds: readonly string[],\n  ): Promise<WorkBuddyCredential | undefined> => {\n    if (!poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).enabled) return undefined\n    const tried = new Set(triedAccountIds)\n    // The saved manual order, so failover honors the user's preference order\n    // (issue #70's expiry guard still outranks it, inside `rankPool`).\n    const order = poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).orderAccountIds\n    for (const row of rankPool(await localPoolMembers(region), Date.now(), order)) {\n      // `excludedBy` carries the reason a measurement rules an account out.\n      // Skipping on it is what makes \"try until none is usable\" mean usable\n      // rather than merely \"not yet tried\": re-hitting an account the upstream\n      // just limited would burn a round trip to learn what we already recorded.\n      if (row.excludedBy !== undefined) continue\n      if (tried.has(row.account.id)) continue\n      const credential = await stacks[region].store.credentialFor(row.account.id)\n      if (credential !== undefined) return credential\n    }\n    return undefined\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Why the pool left a failed request with nowhere to go, in one short clause.\n   *\n   * Runs ONLY after a request has already failed, so it may afford work the hot\n   * path cannot — but it still reads local state only (the probe store and the\n   * member list), because spending a network call to explain a failure would add\n   * a second delay to a request the user is already waiting on.\n   *\n   * It counts rather than lists. The reply is one line inside an error message,\n   * and enumerating accounts there would bury the advice; the card is where a\n   * per-account breakdown belongs, and it already shows one.\n   *\n   * Returns `undefined` when the pool is OFF or has no members — in which case\n   * there is no pool story to tell, and the shim renders its own sentence\n   * without one. That is a real state, not a failure: the plugin supports\n   * running with no pool at all.\n   */\n  const explainPoolShortfall = async (region: WorkBuddyRegion): Promise<string | undefined> => {\n    if (!poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).enabled) return undefined\n    const members = await localPoolMembers(region)\n    if (members.length === 0) return undefined\n    const nowMs = Date.now()\n    // The SAME verdict the ranking uses, asked the same way, so this explanation\n    // cannot contradict the decision that produced the empty search.\n    const excluded = members\n      .map(member => ({ id: member.account.id, excludedBy: exclusionOf(member.probe, nowMs) }))\n      .filter((entry): entry is { id: string, excludedBy: WorkBuddyPoolExclusion } => entry.excludedBy !== undefined)\n    if (excluded.length === 0) {\n      // Nothing is excluded, yet failover found no candidate: every member was\n      // either already tried this request or has no resolvable credential. The\n      // first is the common case and the honest one to report.\n      return '池中其余账号本次请求都已尝试过'\n    }\n    // One clause per DISTINCT reason, with a count, so \"三个账号都在冷却\" reads\n    // as one fact rather than three repetitions of the same sentence.\n    const counts = new Map<WorkBuddyPoolExclusion, number>()\n    for (const entry of excluded) counts.set(entry.excludedBy, (counts.get(entry.excludedBy) ?? 0) + 1)\n    const parts = [...counts.entries()].map(([reason, count]) => `${String(count)} 个${poolExclusionClause(reason)}`)\n    return `池中已无可用账号（${parts.join('、')}）`\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * One exclusion reason as a short Chinese clause for\n   * {@link explainPoolShortfall}.\n   *\n   * Separate from the card's own labels (`exclusion-text.ts` in the client half)\n   * on purpose: those are standalone table cells (\"被限流\"), where these are\n   * glued into a sentence and must read after a count (\"1 个账号被限流\"). Sharing\n   * one string would force one of the two to read wrong.\n   */\n  function poolExclusionClause(reason: WorkBuddyPoolExclusion): string {\n    switch (reason) {\n      case 'rate-limited': return '账号被限流'\n      case 'out-of-credit': return '账号积分耗尽'\n      case 'model-busy': return '账号撞上模型繁忙'\n      case 'credential-rejected': return '账号登录已失效'\n      case 'account-restricted': return '账号被上游限制使用'\n      case 'not-found': return '账号已不存在'\n      case 'unusable': return '账号不可用'\n      default: return '账号不可用'\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The last CREDITS reading per region, so a request can rank by balance\n   * without fetching one.\n   *\n   * Credits are the ranking's second key, and they cost an upstream call per\n   * member. A chat request must never pay that: it happens on the hot path, and\n   * `fetchCredits` per member would turn one page of conversation into N extra\n   * requests. So the request path uses whatever the pool last read (the card's\n   * 60-second poll, a batch test, or a save) and simply skips the key when the\n   * snapshot is too old to speak for today's balances.\n   */\n  const creditsSnapshot: Partial<Record<WorkBuddyRegion, ReadonlyMap<string, WorkBuddyPoolCredits>>> = {}\n  /** How long a credits reading may steer routing. */\n  const CREDITS_SNAPSHOT_MS = 10 * 60_000\n\n  /** When each region's snapshot was taken, for the freshness check above. */\n  const creditsSnapshotAt: Partial<Record<WorkBuddyRegion, number>> = {}\n\n  /**\n   * One region's pool members from LOCAL sources only: the credential store and\n   * the probe store, plus the credits snapshot when it is fresh.\n   *\n   * The request path calls this on every chat completion, so it must not touch\n   * the network. `poolMembersOf` is the honest-but-expensive version (it fetches\n   * credits per member) and stays on the card's route, which is where those\n   * readings come from in the first place.\n   */\n  async function localPoolMembers(region: WorkBuddyRegion): Promise<WorkBuddyPoolMember[]> {\n    const accounts = await poolMemberAccounts(region)\n    const probes = await readPoolProbes(region)\n    const fresh = Date.now() - (creditsSnapshotAt[region] ?? 0) <= CREDITS_SNAPSHOT_MS\n    const credits = fresh ? creditsSnapshot[region] : undefined\n    return accounts.map(account => {\n      const reading = credits?.get(account.id)\n      return {\n        account: {\n          id: account.id,\n          accountName: account.accountName,\n          ...account.enterprise === true ? { enterprise: true } : {},\n        },\n        ...reading === undefined ? {} : { credits: reading },\n        ...probes[account.id] === undefined ? {} : { probe: probes[account.id] as WorkBuddyPoolProbe },\n        tokenExpiresAtMs: account.tokenExpiresAtMs,\n      }\n    })\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Record a failure a LIVE request hit, so the NEXT request starts from a\n   * usable account.\n   *\n   * Without this the ranking only knew what the manual batch test had measured:\n   * an account that had just answered 429 carried no measurement, so\n   * `exclusionOf` returned \"candidate\" and the ranking kept picking it — every\n   * request paid one failed round trip before failing over. Keeping traffic off\n   * an account the upstream just refused is the pool's whole point\n   * (`account-pool.ts` says exactly that about `rate-limited`), and it only\n   * works if live failures count as measurements.\n   *\n   * Deliberately NOT recorded for `client` (HTTP 400): the upstream rejected the\n   * REQUEST, which says nothing about the account, and recording it would\n   * sideline a good account over a bad body.\n   *\n   * The reset time comes from the upstream's own words — the 429 body carries\n   * 「将在 … 重置」 — which `cooldownOf` parses. When it states no time, the store's\n   * own short window applies rather than a guess at a long one.\n   *\n   * THE QUOTA REFRESH POINT IS PASSED IN, and it is not decoration. This path\n   * used to omit `quotaRefreshAtMs` while the PROBE path supplied it, so one\n   * `out-of-credit` failure got two different answers depending on which door it\n   * arrived through: the probe said \"the quota refreshes on the 1st\" (a time the\n   * billing side states outright) while a live chat failure said \"30 minutes\n   * from now\" — a window the account cannot possibly clear, so the pool put it\n   * back and paid another refused round trip. Worse, the write below combines\n   * with the stored record, and the stored refresh point was being LOST in the\n   * process (see {@link mergePoolProbe}).\n   *\n   * Reading it costs one `fetchCredits`, affordable here but not on every\n   * failure: it runs only for `out-of-credit`, where the refresh point is the one\n   * honest answer and there is no cheaper source. Failing to read it is not an\n   * error — the fallback still applies.\n   *\n   * The upstream's text is persisted alongside the outcome so the card can name\n   * the reason. It is REDACTED first because this message is a raw upstream body:\n   * without that, a failure containing a token-shaped string would write it into\n   * a file on disk.\n   */\n  const recordAccountFailure = async (\n    region: WorkBuddyRegion,\n    accountId: string,\n    failure: { status: number, kind: UpstreamErrorKind, message: string, modelId?: string },\n  ): Promise<void> => {\n    if (!poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).enabled) return\n    if (failure.kind === 'client') return\n    // Only accounts this pool covers. Membership is the explicit \"yes, spend\n    // this account's credits\" tick, and a measurement steers routing.\n    const members = poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).memberAccountIds\n    if (members.length > 0 && !members.includes(accountId)) return\n    const outcome = outcomeOfFailure(failure.status, failure.message)\n    // Only for the outcome that HAS a real answer to look up. `quotaRefreshOf`\n    // makes an upstream call, and a rate limit or a transport blip has no\n    // refresh point to find — paying for that read would spend a request to\n    // learn nothing.\n    const quotaRefreshAtMs = outcome === 'out-of-credit'\n      ? await stacks[region].store.credentialFor(accountId)\n        .then(credential => credential === undefined ? undefined : quotaRefreshOf(region, credential))\n        .catch(() => undefined)\n      : undefined\n    const { retryAtMs } = cooldownOf({\n      outcome,\n      retryAfter: null,\n      nowMs: Date.now(),\n      body: failure.message,\n      ...quotaRefreshAtMs === undefined ? {} : { quotaRefreshAtMs },\n    })\n    const message = failure.message === '' ? '' : redactUpstreamText(failure.message)\n    await writePoolProbes(region, {\n      [accountId]: {\n        outcome,\n        atMs: Date.now(),\n        // Named explicitly, because this site OVERWRITES whatever the last\n        // \"test\" measured. Without the label, a user who watched this account\n        // measure `ok` and then found it limited had no way to tell \"a live\n        // request really hit the limit just now\" from \"the file went back to\n        // an older value\" — and the record kept no history to check.\n        source: 'live-request',\n        // WHICH model failed (issue #61). `6004` is account × model, so a live\n        // failure on model A must not read as a statement about model B — nor\n        // erase the fact that a different model once answered.\n        ...failure.modelId === undefined ? {} : { modelId: failure.modelId },\n        ...retryAtMs === undefined ? {} : { retryAtMs },\n        ...message === '' ? {} : { message },\n      },\n    })\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Point this region's store at whoever the pool's ranking says should serve.\n   *\n   * Called once per chat request. With the pool ON the ranking decides the\n   * serving account — the whole point of the switch, and the reason the card can\n   * show a \"current account\" that is not simply the saved selection. With the\n   * pool OFF the override is CLEARED, which restores the user's own choice\n   * immediately: leaving a stale override in place would keep billing under a\n   * switch that reads as off.\n   *\n   * The override is runtime-only and never written to settings, so the user's\n   * recorded choice survives untouched either way — turning the pool off gives\n   * it back verbatim.\n   *\n   * Best-effort: a failure here leaves the previous state in effect, and the\n   * request still goes out (to the user's account when the pool is off, which is\n   * the safe default).\n   */\n  const applyPoolSelection = async (region: WorkBuddyRegion): Promise<void> => {\n    const store = stacks[region].store\n    if (!poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).enabled) {\n      store.setRotatedAccount(undefined)\n      return\n    }\n    const winner = rankPool(await localPoolMembers(region), Date.now(), poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).orderAccountIds)\n      .find(row => row.excludedBy === undefined)\n    // Nobody usable is NOT \"no account\": clearing the override hands the REQUEST\n    // to the saved selection, and the failure is then reported honestly instead of\n    // being masked by a pool with nothing better to offer. (The card does not\n    // present that fallback as healthy — see `servingAccountIsExcluded`.)\n    store.setRotatedAccount(winner?.account.id)\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Whether the account the store would bill is one the pool has measured as\n   * unusable.\n   *\n   * The two questions differ exactly when the pool runs out of usable members: a\n   * REQUEST still has to go somewhere (the saved selection), but the CARD must\n   * not label that account \"in use\" while the same table says it is rate-limited.\n   * One screen contradicting itself is how a user learns to trust neither half —\n   * and \"the pool moved to an available account\" is unverifiable if the display\n   * keeps naming an account the table calls excluded.\n   */\n  const servingAccountIsExcluded = async (region: WorkBuddyRegion): Promise<boolean> => {\n    if (!poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).enabled) return false\n    const credential = await stacks[region].store.current().catch(() => undefined)\n    if (credential === undefined) return false\n    const servingId = workbuddyAccountId(credential)\n    const row = rankPool(await localPoolMembers(region), Date.now(), poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).orderAccountIds)\n      .find(candidate => candidate.account.id === servingId)\n    // Not a member at all (unchecked, or the pool is empty) means the pool has no\n    // opinion, so it must not assert one.\n    return row !== undefined && row.excludedBy !== undefined\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * One region's POOL members, each with its credits and last measurement.\n   *\n   * Only CHECKED accounts: an unchecked account is not a candidate to bill, so\n   * it must not reach the ranking that decides who serves.\n   *\n   * Read through `credentialFor()` — never `resolve()` — so no account's\n   * selection state is touched: a batch must measure every member without\n   * changing which one is billing. Credits are fetched per account and a\n   * failure degrades to \"unknown\" for that row only — one unreadable balance\n   * must not blank the whole pool.\n   */\n  async function poolMembersOf(region: WorkBuddyRegion): Promise<WorkBuddyPoolMember[]> {\n    const store = stacks[region].store\n    const accounts = await poolMemberAccounts(region)\n    const probes = await readPoolProbes(region)\n    const rows = await Promise.all(accounts.map(async account => {\n      const credential = await store.credentialFor(account.id).catch(() => undefined)\n      const credits = credential === undefined\n        ? undefined\n        : await client.fetchCredits(credential).catch(() => undefined)\n      return {\n        account: {\n          id: account.id,\n          accountName: account.accountName,\n          ...account.enterprise === true ? { enterprise: true } : {},\n        },\n        ...credits === undefined ? {} : {\n          credits: {\n            // The upstream client already aggregates these two, and the card\n            // displays the same figures — so re-deriving them here would let\n            // the pool and the card disagree about one account's expiry.\n            total: credits.total,\n            expiringSoon: credits.expiringSoon,\n            ...credits.nearestExpiryMs === undefined\n              ? {}\n              : { nearestExpiryMs: credits.nearestExpiryMs },\n          },\n        },\n        ...probes[account.id] === undefined ? {} : { probe: probes[account.id] as WorkBuddyPoolProbe },\n        tokenExpiresAtMs: account.tokenExpiresAtMs,\n      }\n    }))\n    // Remember the balances for the REQUEST path, which cannot afford to fetch\n    // them (`localPoolMembers`). Written here because this is the one place the\n    // pool pays for a credits reading — the card's poll and every batch test run\n    // through it — so the snapshot is exactly as fresh as the numbers the user\n    // is looking at.\n    creditsSnapshot[region] = new Map(\n      rows\n        .filter(row => row.credits !== undefined)\n        .map(row => [row.account.id, row.credits as WorkBuddyPoolCredits]),\n    )\n    creditsSnapshotAt[region] = Date.now()\n    return rows\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The model a per-row test would use right now: the SAME resolution the\n   * batch route performs (`resolveTargetModel` over the region's displayed\n   * catalog and the saved preference — including a per-account override,\n   * issue #61), so a row test and a batch test never measure different models.\n   * Undefined mirrors the route's 409 states.\n   */\n  const regionTargetModel = (region: WorkBuddyRegion): string | undefined => {\n    const target = resolveTargetModel(\n      displayModels(current(), region),\n      poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).targetModelId,\n    )\n    return target.modelId\n  }\n\n  /** One region's pool preferences, with the schema's defaults applied. */\n  /**\n   * WHERE this region's credit readout sits (issue #62).\n   *\n   * A local reader rather than a schema default so a config written before the\n   * setting existed reads as `composer` — the seat that was the only one, so an\n   * upgrade moves nothing on screen.\n   */\n  const creditPositionOf = (config: Config, region: WorkBuddyRegion): WorkBuddyCreditPosition =>\n    regionStateOf(config, region).creditPosition === 'stats' ? 'stats' : 'composer'\n\n  const poolPreferencesOf = (config: Config, region: WorkBuddyRegion) => {\n    const pool = regionStateOf(config, region).pool\n    const probeInputTokens = pool?.probeInputTokens\n    return {\n      enabled: pool?.enabled === true,\n      targetModelId: pool?.targetModelId ?? '',\n      // Passed through as a plain copy: the config value is a live cosmokit\n      // reference on the 0.1.7 line, and handing that to a route would let a\n      // settings mutation change what a running request resolves against.\n      // Passed through RAW (not coerced here): `resolveProbeInputTokens` owns\n      // that decision and is the only place that knows the offered set. Coercing\n      // in two places is how a menu and its validation drift apart.\n      //\n      // Spread rather than assigned, because `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` makes\n      // an explicit `undefined` a different thing from an absent key — and the\n      // consumers of this type rely on absence meaning \"never chose\".\n      ...probeInputTokens === undefined ? {} : { probeInputTokens },\n      memberAccountIds: pool?.memberAccountIds ?? [],\n      orderAccountIds: pool?.orderAccountIds ?? [],\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The accounts the user has checked into this region's pool.\n   *\n   * Membership is an EXPLICIT opt-in list, not \"every local sign-in\": the two\n   * batch actions spend real credits and claim real rewards, so which accounts\n   * they touch has to be the user's decision rather than a side effect of\n   * having signed in on this machine. An account that is not checked is\n   * untouched by check-in, testing, and rotation alike.\n   */\n  async function poolMemberAccounts(region: WorkBuddyRegion) {\n    const saved = poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).memberAccountIds\n    if (saved.length === 0) return []\n    const accounts = await stacks[region].store.accounts()\n    const byId = new Map(accounts.map(account => [account.id, account]))\n    // The shared rule ANSWERS this question and this site only supplies the\n    // store's order (see `effectiveMembersOf`'s contract) plus the id→row\n    // lookup. The obvious shape — filter `accounts` by a set built from the\n    // rule — is deceptive: it makes the delegation unobservable, because\n    // `accounts.filter(id ∈ (store ∩ saved))` collapses to `accounts ∩ saved`\n    // for ANY membership rule at all. That version passed every test while the\n    // rule could have been deleted; the independent verification proved it. So\n    // the rule's own output drives the result.\n    return effectiveMembersOf(\n      accounts.map(account => account.id),\n      new Set(saved),\n    ).flatMap(id => {\n      const account = byId.get(id)\n      return account === undefined ? [] : [account]\n    })\n  }\n\n  /** The batch runner's view of one region's pool members. */\n  async function poolTargets(region: WorkBuddyRegion): Promise<WorkBuddyPoolTarget[]> {\n    const accounts = await poolMemberAccounts(region)\n    return accounts.map(account => ({\n      accountId: account.id,\n      accountName: account.accountName,\n      // Enterprise members have no consumer check-in (issue #124); the runner\n      // skips them instead of spending a request on a refusal it can predict.\n      ...account.enterprise === true ? { enterprise: true } : {},\n    }))\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The two actions' shared dependency set.\n   *\n   * `credentialFor` — never `resolve()` — is what makes a batch possible at\n   * all: it fetches any account's credential WITHOUT changing the region's\n   * selection, so testing every account cannot switch the account that bills\n   * the user's live traffic.\n   */\n  function poolRunnerDeps(region: WorkBuddyRegion): WorkBuddyPoolRunnerDeps {\n    const store = stacks[region].store\n    return {\n      credentialFor: accountId => store.credentialFor(accountId),\n      fetchCheckinStatus: credential => client.fetchCheckinStatus(credential),\n      claimDailyCheckin: credential => client.claimDailyCheckin(credential),\n      probe: async (credential, modelId) => {\n        // The SAME quota-refresh input the single-model probe route passes.\n        // Without it an exhausted account reports \"the upstream gave no time\",\n        // so the card could not say when a drained account returns — and the\n        // pool would re-test it blindly instead of waiting for the reset. The\n        // read is best-effort: a probe without a cooldown is still a useful\n        // probe (see `quotaRefreshOf`).\n        const quotaRefreshAtMs = await quotaRefreshOf(region, credential)\n        // The user's chosen probe size, coerced against the offered set. Read\n        // HERE rather than captured when these deps were built, so changing the\n        // setting takes effect on the very next test rather than the next reload.\n        const inputTokens = resolveProbeInputTokens(\n          poolPreferencesOf(current(), region).probeInputTokens,\n        )\n        return probeModel({\n          client,\n          credential,\n          modelId,\n          nowMs: Date.now(),\n          inputTokens,\n          ...quotaRefreshAtMs === undefined ? {} : { quotaRefreshAtMs },\n        })\n      },\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Settings registration (0.1.7+ only).\n  // `SettingsForms` dropped `installSection` entirely and exposes\n  // `configure({auto}, owner)`; calling the removed method unconditionally made\n  // `apply()` throw on 0.1.7 (`ctx.settings.installSection is not a function`)\n  // and took down the WHOLE plugin. Since 2.1.0 the plugin supports DSH\n  // 0.1.7-rc.1 and up only, so `configure` is the sole path and is called\n  // unconditionally — the pre-0.1.7 `SettingsProvider.installSection` branch\n  // is gone with the line it served.\n  //\n  // The call goes through a narrow local type rather than a blanket `any`: the\n  // installed typings describe the 0.1.5 line only, so `configure` is not on\n  // `SettingsProvider` and the event names below are not in `Events`. Naming\n  // the shapes here keeps the widening honest and reviewable.\n  interface SettingsShapes {\n    configure: (presentation: { auto?: boolean }, owner?: unknown) => () => void\n  }\n\n  // `inject` rather than a direct read: `settings` is an optional service, and\n  // the callback runs once it is actually present. `configure` returns a\n  // disposer that must be registered with the calling plugin's effects, or the\n  // presentation policy leaks past disposal (the first-party plugins do the\n  // same: `child.effect(() => child.settings.configure({ auto: false }, …))`).\n  ctx.inject(['settings'], (sctx) => {\n    const settings = sctx.settings as unknown as SettingsShapes\n    ctx.effect(() => settings.configure({ auto: true }, ctx.fiber))\n  })\n\n  // 0.1.7 hands volatile values back as live references and announces each\n  // write on this event, so the card's selections are re-read after one. The\n  // event does not exist on 0.1.5, which never emits it.\n  ;(ctx as unknown as { on(name: string, listener: () => void): unknown })\n    .on('loader/volatile-update', () => {\n      applySelection(current())\n    })\n\n  // Initial wiring: selections, per-region catalogs from the saved state.\n  applySelection(config)\n\n  // Attribute the legacy single-account selection to its own region once the\n  // local scan can tell which one that is, then re-apply. Until this resolves\n  // (or when no legacy field exists) both regions simply run their defaults.\n  void (async () => {\n    try {\n      const region = await legacyAttributionRegion(current(), async candidate =>\n        stacks[candidate].store.accounts())\n      if (region === undefined) {\n        // Either no legacy field, or the saved account vanished (the app\n        // replaced its sign-in): both regions keep their defaults and the card\n        // lets the user re-select.\n        return\n      }\n      legacyAccountRegion = region\n      applySelection(current())\n    } catch {\n      // Scan failure: keep defaults; the next card-driven scan converges.\n    }\n  })()\n\n  let stopped = false\n\n  ctx.effect(() => () => {\n    stopped = true\n    for (const region of REGION_KEYS) void stacks[region].shim.close()\n    void clearHostHeartbeat()\n  })\n\n  void Promise.all(REGION_KEYS.map(region => stacks[region].shim.ready))\n    .then(async () => {\n      if (stopped) return\n\n      const adapters = {} as Record<WorkBuddyRegion, WorkBuddyAdapter>\n      // The host services that can fold history are optional: a host without the\n      // compaction bundle loaded simply has no `compaction` service, and the\n      // rescue then reports \"nothing folded\" instead of failing a request.\n      // Resolved per attempt rather than captured, because plugin load order\n      // decides when each service exists.\n      const hostService = <Service>(name: string): Service | undefined =>\n        (ctx as unknown as { get(service: string): unknown }).get(name) as Service | undefined\n      const contextRescue = createContextRescue(() => ({\n        meter: hostService<RescueTokenMeter>('tokenMeter'),\n        compaction: hostService<RescueCompaction>('compaction'),\n        agents: hostService<RescueAgents>('agents'),\n        log: (message: string, error?: unknown) => { ctx.logger.warn(message, error) },\n      }))\n      try {\n        // Constructed only once the listeners hold their ports: a provider's\n        // models read the shim origin at construction time.\n        for (const region of REGION_KEYS) {\n          adapters[region] = createWorkBuddyAdapter({\n            shim: stacks[region].shim,\n            store: stacks[region].store,\n            catalog: stacks[region].catalog,\n            provider: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDERS[region],\n            displayName: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER_DISPLAY_NAMES[region],\n            resolveAttachments: () => ctx.get('attachments'),\n            resolveContextRescue: () => contextRescue,\n            /**\n             * Issue #179: the per-model default tier, read LIVE for the same\n             * reason as the switch below — the card saves it mid-session, and a\n             * captured map would keep dispatching the old tier until a reload.\n             *\n             * `current()` rather than the captured `config` is what makes a save\n             * take effect on the very next request; `applySelection` still runs\n             * `invalidateCatalog()` on the same write, which is what re-publishes\n             * the advertised `defaultEffort` to the host's model catalog.\n             */\n            defaultThinkingEfforts: () => regionStateOf(current(), region).defaultThinkingEfforts ?? {},\n            // Read through `current()` (not the captured `config`) so the\n            // switch applies to the NEXT request: the user turns it on while\n            // looking at a working-notes turn, and a stale capture would make\n            // that look like a broken toggle.\n            dropForeignThinking: () => unwrapVolatile(current().dropForeignThinking) === true,\n            onForeignThinking: blocks => {\n              latestForeignThinking = { blocks, atMs: Date.now() }\n              // Cross-provider blocks are dropped even when the switch is off.\n              // Name that edit in the log: it changes bytes the upstream would\n              // otherwise count, and the user may be looking for the reason\n              // their transcript lost the other provider's working notes.\n              if (unwrapVolatile(current().dropForeignThinking) !== true) {\n                ctx.logger.warn(\n                  `dsh-connect-workbuddy: dropped ${blocks.length} cross-provider thinking block(s) `\n                  + 'before a WorkBuddy request (provider switch); see issue #163',\n                )\n              }\n            },\n          })\n        }\n        invalidateCatalog = () => {\n          for (const region of REGION_KEYS) adapters[region].invalidate()\n        }\n\n        let releaseAdapterCn: (() => void) | undefined\n        let releaseAdapterGlobal: (() => void) | undefined\n        let releaseDirectory: (() => void) | undefined\n        try {\n          // Always register the route first: an empty INITIAL registration is\n          // invalid (`INVALID_ADAPTER`), while `replace([])` on a live one is\n          // explicitly legal. `syncRegionRegistration` below then withdraws the\n          // route for a region the user has switched off, in the same synchronous\n          // section, so nothing observes the transient route.\n          releaseAdapterCn = registration.cn.adapter = ctx.llm.registerAdapter([WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER], adapters.cn.adapter)\n          releaseAdapterGlobal = registration.global.adapter = ctx.llm.registerAdapter([WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER], adapters.global.adapter)\n          releaseDirectory = registration.cn.directory = ctx.llm.registerConfigurableProviders([\n            {\n              provider: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER,\n              displayName: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER_DISPLAY_NAMES.cn,\n              settingsNs,\n              settingsPath: [],\n              declared: false,\n            },\n            {\n              provider: WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER,\n              displayName: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER_DISPLAY_NAMES.global,\n              settingsNs,\n              settingsPath: [],\n              declared: false,\n            },\n          ])\n        } finally {\n          if (releaseAdapterCn === undefined || releaseAdapterGlobal === undefined || releaseDirectory === undefined) {\n            // Registration threw; release whichever half landed.\n            releaseAdapterCn?.()\n            releaseAdapterGlobal?.()\n            releaseDirectory?.()\n          }\n        }\n        try {\n          ctx.effect(() => () => {\n            releaseAdapterCn?.()\n            releaseAdapterGlobal?.()\n            releaseDirectory?.()\n          })\n        } catch {\n          // The plugin was disposed during registration; release immediately —\n          // the plugin-level disposer already closed the shims.\n          releaseAdapterCn?.()\n          releaseAdapterGlobal?.()\n          releaseDirectory?.()\n        }\n\n        // Converge both regions on the saved on/off state: a region switched off\n        // while the harness was down is withdrawn here, before the startup seed\n        // below. `syncRegionRegistration` is a no-op for the freshly registered\n        // routes until this very call populates their handles.\n        syncRegionRegistration(current())\n\n        ctx.llm.registerModelDiscovery(settingsNs, async (request, signal) => {\n          const region = regionOfProvider(request.provider ?? '')\n          if (region === undefined) return []\n          // A switched-off region advertises nothing: its route is withdrawn, so\n          // this is defence in depth against a stale model-picker refresh.\n          if (!regionEnabled(current(), region)) return []\n          const discovered = await discoverModels(region, signal)\n          // Load levels are stamped on the RUNTIME roster only — see\n          // `withBusyLevels` for why the settings draft stays free of them.\n          const live = await withBusyLevels(region, discovered, signal)\n          const state = regionStateOf(current(), region)\n          const next = withImageSelection(\n            deriveCatalog(\n              live,\n              new Set(state.enabledModelIds ?? []),\n              state.contextBudgets ?? {},\n            ),\n            // #168: a discovery read is exactly the path that reveals a NEW\n            // model, so an unedited region must be seeded from the table here\n            // rather than from the array a previous refresh wrote.\n            effectiveImageIds(state, live),\n          )\n          return next.map(model => ({\n            id: model.id,\n            name: workBuddyDisplayName(model),\n            contextWindow: model.contextWindow,\n            maxTokens: model.maxTokens,\n            inputModalities: workBuddyModelInput(model),\n          }))\n        })\n\n        // The host bundle is live: write a heartbeat so the status CLI can\n        // report host health without a browser. Cleared on disposal; a stale\n        // heartbeat after a crash is detected by PID in the reader.\n        void writeHostHeartbeat()\n      } catch (error: unknown) {\n        ctx.logger.error('dsh-connect-workbuddy: provider registration failed', error)\n        return\n      }\n\n      // Seed each region's catalog from that region's selected account (or the\n      // live sign-in default when nothing is selected yet).\n      if (stopped) return\n\n      // Heal the FIELD before anything depends on the other write paths: the\n      // catalog repair below also cleans it, but only after a live roster lands\n      // (so never for a disabled region, an offline start, or an empty answer).\n      // The boot does not wait for it — the write only changes the file's shape,\n      // and the read side is already healed in memory — but the repair below\n      // DOES await it, because two full-field writers racing could otherwise\n      // land the older directory last and undo the repair for this boot.\n      const regionsFieldHealed = healRegionsFieldIfCorrupt()\n\n      for (const region of REGION_KEYS) {\n        // A switched-off region is skipped entirely: its route is withdrawn, so\n        // the request would be pure waste — and skipping it is also what keeps a\n        // disabled region from contributing an error to the log on every start.\n        if (!regionEnabled(current(), region)) continue\n        void (async () => {\n          try {\n            const credential = await stacks[region].store.resolve()\n            if (stopped) return\n            const models = await withBusyLevels(\n              region,\n              await client.fetchModels(\n                credential,\n                undefined,\n                regionStateOf(current(), region).enabledModelIds ?? [],\n              ),\n            )\n            if (stopped) return\n            const state = regionStateOf(current(), region)\n            stacks[region].catalog.set(withEffectiveOff(\n              withImageSelection(\n                deriveCatalog(models, new Set(state.enabledModelIds ?? []), state.contextBudgets ?? {}),\n                // #168: the startup seed is the FIFTH writer of `multimodal` and\n                // the one that decides what the picker shows for a whole\n                // session. Seeding it from the stored array is what froze a\n                // newly-added verified-multimodal model at `['text']`.\n                effectiveImageIds(state, models),\n              ),\n              new Set(savedOffModelIds(state, models)),\n            ))\n            adapters[region].invalidate()\n            // Record when THIS machine first saw each id of the roster that just\n            // landed — the whole \"· N 新模型\" story. This is the ONLY writer of\n            // that history; the usage route answering the pill is read-only, so\n            // a panel poll can never change \"who is new\".\n            //\n            // A failure is a warning, NOT a reason to fall back to the static\n            // list (the catalog above is already set), and it is logged with its\n            // reason rather than silently answered as \"no new models\": those are\n            // two entirely different facts.\n            try {\n              await stacks[region].rosterSeen.note(models.map(model => model.id))\n            } catch (error: unknown) {\n              ctx.logger.warn(`dsh-connect-workbuddy: ${region} first-sighting history not updated`, error)\n            }\n            // `lastCatalog` is deliberately NOT seeded here: it belongs to the\n            // user's saved selection, written only by the card's explicit save\n            // (via settingsScope). Until then the card shows the live fallback\n            // directory and one press of \"Refresh\" captures the real one.\n            //\n            // BUT A STALE DIRECTORY IS REPAIRED (issues #114/#115). A directory\n            // written by an older build — or one that a cold start seeded with\n            // the static fallback table — would keep the roster frozen at that\n            // snapshot, and the card's next save would write it back. So the\n            // directory is healed against the roster that just landed. The\n            // repair writes only when the fingerprint differs, so a profile\n            // already in agreement is never touched.\n            //\n            // The shape heal goes first, so the directory this describes is the\n            // CLEANED one: the repair reads the field through `describe()` right\n            // before writing, and awaiting here is what keeps the two writers\n            // from landing out of order.\n            await regionsFieldHealed\n            await repairRegionCatalog(region, models)\n          } catch (error: unknown) {\n            ctx.logger.warn(\n              `dsh-connect-workbuddy: dynamic ${region} model catalog unavailable; serving the static fallback list`,\n              error,\n            )\n          }\n        })()\n      }\n    })\n    .catch((error: unknown) => {\n      ctx.logger.error('dsh-connect-workbuddy: loopback endpoint failed to start; providers not registered', error)\n    })\n}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "src/adapter.ts",
      "content": "/**\n * The WorkBuddy pi-ai providers: loopback-backed adapters registered\n * into the Harness LLM seam, assembled from public `dsh-llm-pi-ai`\n * extension points. One instance per region — `workbuddy` for the domestic\n * gateway, `workbuddy-global` for the international one — each pointing at\n * its own shim and catalog so the two regions serve simultaneously.\n *\n * 参考：corrinehu/dsh-workbuddy-connect（MIT，Copyright (c) 2026 Corrine Hu）\n *   — pi-ai provider 的装配方式（createProvider + openAICompletionsApi +\n *     inert auth plane + 用 shim 的进程内 secret 作为 apiKey）由该项目实现；\n *   DSH 插件结构与 provider 注册的思路参照\n *     franksong2702/dsh-codex-connect（Apache-2.0），经其转引。\n * 改动：模型描述符补上 upstream 给出的多模态与推理档位信息（若有），\n *   供 DSH 的能力判断使用；工厂参数化 provider id，支持双区域实例。\n *\n * @module dsh-connect-workbuddy/adapter\n */\n\nimport { createAssistantMessageEventStream, createProvider, isContextOverflow } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai'\nimport type { Api, AssistantMessage, AssistantMessageEvent, AssistantMessageEventStream, AuthContext, Context, CredentialStore, Message, Model, Provider, ProviderStreams } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai'\nimport { openAICompletionsApi } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai/api/openai-completions.lazy'\nimport { ReasoningEffortId, resolveRetryPolicy } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm'\nimport type { GenerateOptions, LlmModelReasoningInfo, LlmResolvedModelInfo, PreparedAdapterCall, StreamChunk } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm'\nimport { PiAiAdapter } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm-pi-ai'\nimport type { PiAiAdapterOptions, ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm-pi-ai'\nimport type { AttachmentStore } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-attachment'\nimport type { WorkBuddyCredentialStore } from './auth.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyCatalog, WorkBuddyModelInfo } from './catalog.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyShim } from './shim.ts'\nimport { offRefused } from './off-thinking.ts'\nimport { defaultEffortFor } from './default-effort.ts'\nimport { WORKBUDDY_DEFAULT_EFFORT } from './default-effort.ts'\nimport { WORKBUDDY_FALLBACK_SYSTEM_PROMPT } from './upstream.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyRegion } from './upstream.ts'\nimport { piAiRuntimeInfo } from './pi-ai-runtime.ts'\nimport type { PiAiGeneration } from './pi-ai-runtime.ts'\nimport type { ContextRescue } from './context-rescue.ts'\n\n/**\n * The tier a request gets when it names none — compaction summaries, above all\n * (issue #83). See the `reasoning` field in `profileFor` for why an absent\n * default is not \"no opinion\" but \"whatever pi-ai's level map says\", which is\n * how `off` became the silent default for `/compact`.\n *\n * Re-exported from `./default-effort.ts`, which is where issue #179 put the\n * per-model override: this constant is now the route-wide FALLBACK for a model\n * the user has not picked a tier for, not the final answer for the roster. The\n * name and value are unchanged so every existing caller and test still reads.\n */\nexport { WORKBUDDY_DEFAULT_EFFORT } from './default-effort.ts'\n\n/** Provider route this bundle owns for the domestic (CN) gateway. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER = 'workbuddy'\n\n/** Provider route this bundle owns for the international gateway. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER = 'workbuddy-global'\n\n/** The provider id each region registers as. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_PROVIDERS: Readonly<Record<WorkBuddyRegion, string>> = {\n  cn: WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER,\n  global: WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER,\n}\n\n/**\n * Model identity BOTH regions stamp on their models — the fix for issue #59.\n *\n * pi-ai keeps a historical thinking block as `thinking` only when the message\n * that carried it names the SAME provider, api and model id as the request in\n * flight (`transform-messages.js:68`); every other block is downgraded to plain\n * `text` (`:88–96`) — downgraded, not dropped, which is why no byte count ever\n * explained the 1.94× regional jump. The two gateways are two provider STRINGS\n * for ONE model, so a transcript recorded in one region was replayed as text in\n * the other, and issue #59's reporter measured exactly that: 708K → 1.374M on\n * byte-identical history, and a compression request that could then never fit\n * the window it was computed for.\n *\n * The stamp is NOT inert, and reading it as inert is what broke issue #75.\n * `provider` is the KEY pi-ai dispatches on: `requireProvider` resolves\n * `this.providers.get(model.provider)` and throws\n * `Unknown provider: ${model.provider}` when it is absent (`models.js:410–416`\n * on the host's 1.1.0, `:351–357` on the 0.85.1 this repo resolves), and\n * `getAuth` resolves the same key, answering `Provider is not configured`\n * instead (`:334–341` and `:429`; `:275–280` and `:366` on 0.85.1). Both sit on\n * the LIVE chat path: `dsh-llm-pi-ai` streams through\n * `snapshot.models.streamSimple(model, …)` (`lib/index.js:1983`), which calls\n * `requireProvider` before a byte is sent (`models.js:454–459`). Each region\n * builds its OWN registry from its own adapter — `current()` registers\n * `profile.piProvider` per profile (`lib/index.js:1852–1862`) — so the\n * `workbuddy-global` region registered only `workbuddy-global` and could not\n * dispatch a model stamped `workbuddy`: every request failed with\n * `Unknown provider: workbuddy` before reaching the gateway.\n * {@link modelIdentityKeysOf} is the correction: the identity has to be a\n * registry key of its own in every region, or the stamp cannot be shared.\n *\n * Lookup by ROUTE is a separate question and stays intact: `getModel(provider,\n * id)` is `getModels(provider).find(...)` (`models.js:116–118`), i.e. it goes\n * through the map key, while the STAMPED field is whatever the provider put\n * there. So the route stays `workbuddy`/`workbuddy-global` (each with its own\n * shim, catalog and credential store) while every model it serves advertises\n * THIS id. The openai-completions api reads `model.provider` only for\n * provider-specific quirks (none of which match these ids) and as an\n * error-message label.\n *\n * Deliberately NOT the same string as the global route: a message is only\n * same-model when provider, api AND model id all match, so the shared id must\n * not be reachable as a route a user could pick — it names one model, not one\n * gateway. It IS reachable as a pi-ai registry key, which is the point: those\n * are internal and never surface as a route, because `registerAdapter` takes\n * the route list explicitly (`dsh-llm/lib/index.js:1842–1861`,\n * `prepareRoutes` `:1867–1885`) and this plugin passes exactly the two region\n * ids (`src/index.ts:2069–2086`).\n */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY = 'workbuddy'\n\n/**\n * The api string `dsh-llm-pi-ai` stamps on a replayed message it could not\n * rebuild natively (`foreignAssistant()`), i.e. a message that went through the\n * host's fallback path rather than this plugin's own request builder.\n *\n * It is the ONLY api value {@link alignAssistantIdentities} repairs, because it\n * is the only one that proves the field is a host marker instead of a shape this\n * plugin really produced.\n */\nexport const HOST_FOREIGN_API = 'dsh-foreign'\n\n/**\n * Registry keys an adapter serving `providerId` must publish so that every\n * stamp its models carry resolves inside its own region (issue #75).\n *\n * The route id first, then the model identity, de-duplicated — route-first so\n * `profiles()` opens with the route the adapter actually serves. CN yields one\n * key (`workbuddy` is both its route and the shared identity) and the global\n * region yields two (`workbuddy-global`, `workbuddy`); the two regions\n * therefore publish different key sets, and each must publish the shared one.\n *\n * `identity` is a parameter because {@link WorkBuddyAdapterOptions.modelIdentity}\n * can override the stamp: the keys have to follow the stamp, or a test that\n * stamps a per-region id would get the production keys back.\n */\nexport function modelIdentityKeysOf(\n  providerId: string | undefined,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set([providerId, identity].filter((key): key is string => key !== undefined))]\n}\n\n/** Region a provider route id belongs to. */\nexport function regionOfProvider(provider: string): WorkBuddyRegion | undefined {\n  for (const [region, id] of Object.entries(WORKBUDDY_PROVIDERS) as [WorkBuddyRegion, string][]) {\n    if (id === provider) return region\n  }\n  return undefined\n}\n\n/** Human-readable provider name, shown in the DSH model picker. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER_DISPLAY_NAMES: Readonly<Record<WorkBuddyRegion, string>> = {\n  cn: 'WorkBuddy',\n  global: 'WorkBuddy Global',\n}\n\n/** Provider idle ceiling while one stream read is outstanding. */\nexport const WORKBUDDY_STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000\n\n/**\n * Image-request budgets at the dsh-llm-pi-ai defaults; the profile type made\n * them required in 0.1.1-rc.2.\n */\nconst REQUEST_IMAGE_BUDGETS = {\n  maxRequestImageBytes: 20_971_520,\n  requestImagePixelBudget: 4_194_304,\n  requestImageMaxBytes: 1_048_576,\n} as const\n\n/**\n * Inert pi-ai auth plane. The workbuddy route authenticates only through the\n * shim shared secret resolved per request by `resolveApiKey`, so pi-ai's own\n * credential lifecycle and ambient discovery must never manufacture a\n * credential for it. `PiAiAdapterOptions.auth` is required since 0.1.1-rc.2;\n * every ambient question here answers \"nothing stored, nothing set\".\n */\nconst INERT_AUTH: { credentials: CredentialStore; authContext: AuthContext } = {\n  credentials: {\n    async read() { return undefined },\n    async list() { return [] },\n    async modify() {\n      throw new Error('dsh-connect-workbuddy: the workbuddy route has no pi-ai credential lifecycle')\n    },\n    async delete() {},\n  },\n  authContext: {\n    async env() { return undefined },\n    async fileExists() { return false },\n  },\n}\n\n/** No per-token pricing is knowable for a subscription quota; report zero. */\nconst NO_COST = { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 } as const\n\n/** Constructor dependencies. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyAdapterOptions {\n  shim: WorkBuddyShim\n  store: WorkBuddyCredentialStore\n  catalog: WorkBuddyCatalog\n  /** Provider route id this instance serves; defaults to the CN route. */\n  provider?: string\n  /** pi-ai provider name and profile display name; defaults to the CN name. */\n  displayName?: string\n  /**\n   * Model identity stamped on every model this route serves; defaults to\n   * {@link WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY}. Tests override it to model a transcript\n   * recorded by a host that still stamped per-region ids.\n   *\n   * It also decides the extra registry key this adapter publishes alongside\n   * its route (issue #75) — see {@link modelIdentityKeysOf}, which is what\n   * keeps an overridden stamp resolvable exactly like the production one.\n   */\n  modelIdentity?: string\n  /** Resolve the durable attachment service at request time, when present. */\n  resolveAttachments?: () => AttachmentStore | undefined\n  /**\n   * Resolve the host-side context-overflow rescue, when this host exposes one.\n   * Omitted (or resolving to `undefined`) leaves the adapter exactly as it was:\n   * a provider overflow is forwarded and the host's own recovery decides.\n   */\n  resolveContextRescue?: () => ContextRescue | undefined\n  /**\n   * Override the pi-ai generation gate (see {@link piAiRuntimeInfo}); tests\n   * inject both branches because this checkout only ever RESOLVES one of\n   * them. Production callers leave it unset.\n   */\n  piAiGeneration?: PiAiGeneration\n  /**\n   * Drop SAME-ROUTE foreign thinking blocks pi-ai would publish as prose\n   * (issue #147).\n   *\n   * CROSS-PROVIDER turns are dropped unconditionally: after a provider switch\n   * the host rebuilds the other provider's assistant turns as `dsh-foreign`,\n   * their unsigned thinking blocks can never be replayed, and pi-ai would\n   * otherwise flatten them into assistant prose that the WorkBuddy gateway\n   * refuses with 11128 (issue #163's measured scene). This switch only widens\n   * the repair to a model switch INSIDE one of this bundle's own routes.\n   *\n   * OFF by default for that same-route leg, and read LIVE (a getter, not a\n   * boolean) so the switch takes effect on the next request without reloading\n   * the plugin: turning it on mid-session is the whole point — the user sees\n   * the working-notes turn, flips the switch, and the next turn is answered\n   * normally.\n   */\n  dropForeignThinking?: () => boolean\n  /**\n   * Told which foreign thinking blocks a request dropped, so the reply can name\n   * the condition instead of leaving the user to conclude the model broke\n   * (issue #147 asks for at least a visible hint). Called at most once per\n   * request; failures are swallowed.\n   */\n  onForeignThinking?: (blocks: readonly ForeignThinkingBlock[]) => void\n  /**\n   * The per-model default thinking tier the user saved (issue #179), keyed by\n   * model id.\n   *\n   * A GETTER, not a captured map: the choice is a setting the user can change\n   * mid-session, and a captured copy would keep dispatching the old tier until\n   * a reload. Production reads the live region slot; tests pass a literal.\n   *\n   * An absent key means \"follow the upstream's own `defaultEffort`\" — see\n   * `src/default-effort.ts` for how that is resolved against the model's real\n   * vocabulary, which is what keeps a stale saved tier from ever reaching the\n   * wire as an `UNSUPPORTED_REASONING_EFFORT`.\n   */\n  defaultThinkingEfforts?: () => Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>\n}\n\n/** What {@link createWorkBuddyAdapter} hands back. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyAdapter {\n  adapter: PiAiAdapter\n  /** Rebuild the adapter's provider snapshot; call after a catalog update. */\n  invalidate: () => void\n}\n\nconst THINKING_LEVELS = ['minimal', 'low', 'medium', 'high', 'xhigh', 'max'] as const\n\ntype WorkBuddyThinkingLevel = typeof THINKING_LEVELS[number]\ntype WorkBuddyThinkingLevelMap = Partial<Record<'off' | WorkBuddyThinkingLevel, string | null>>\n\n/** pi-ai input modalities: images only when WorkBuddy advertises them. */\nexport function workBuddyModelInput(info: WorkBuddyModelInfo): ('text' | 'image')[] {\n  return info.multimodal === true ? ['text', 'image'] : ['text']\n}\n\n/**\n * DSH-facing display name: the model name plus the upstream credit multiplier,\n * spelled the way WorkBuddy's own selector does (`GLM-5.3 · x0.79`).\n *\n * Display-only by construction: every DSH-side join keys on the model id —\n * the selector's current choice (`provider` + `model`), the durable\n * `model/selection` / `request/header` session events, the agent default-model\n * settings, and the request wire (`model: <id>` reaching the shim). A model\n * without a parsed multiplier keeps its bare name; a zero multiplier shows\n * `x0.00`, matching WorkBuddy's rendering of free models.\n */\nexport function workBuddyDisplayName(info: WorkBuddyModelInfo): string {\n  const base = info.creditMultiplier === undefined\n    ? info.name\n    : `${info.name} · x${info.creditMultiplier.toFixed(2)}`\n  // Load does NOT ride on this string any more. The host renders the model\n  // name verbatim, and the plugin now draws the load badge in the host's own\n  // picker through the `conversation.input.model.label` slot\n  // (`src/client/WorkBuddyModelLabel.tsx`) — where it can be a COLOURED figure\n  // rather than a character whose colour belongs to the emoji font. Leaving\n  // the emoji here as well would show the same fact twice, in two visual\n  // languages. See `src/busy.ts`.\n  return base\n}\n\n/**\n * Map only levels advertised by WorkBuddy; undeclared DSH levels stay unavailable.\n *\n * `off` is offered EXACTLY when `canDisableThinking === true` and withheld\n * (mapped to `null`) otherwise. `null` is pi-ai's \"this level is not\n * supported\": `getSupportedThinkingLevels` filters on `mapped === null`, and\n * `openai-completions` reads `thinkingLevelMap?.off !== null` to decide whether\n * it may send a thinking-disabled request.\n *\n * This condition used to be inverted (`!== true` set the null), which locked\n * `off` OFF for precisely the models that CAN disable thinking while leaving it\n * ON for those that cannot. It stayed invisible because the only test probed\n * the `canDisableThinking: false` half, which the inverted form also passes.\n *\n * The value read here is already the EFFECTIVE one: the runtime catalog is\n * stamped by `withEffectiveOff()` before it reaches the adapter, so this is the\n * user's `off` selection when they made one — except for the ids on\n * `REFUSES_OFF_MODEL_IDS`, which the stamp forces to `false` no matter what the\n * user asked for (issue #83: this level, once ticked, also became the IMPLICIT\n * default for every request that carries no effort at all, which is how\n * compaction went to 100% failure). The table is re-checked here as well so the\n * wall does not depend on every caller remembering to stamp first.\n *\n * Absence is \"unknown\" and stays unoffered, matching how the rest of the plugin\n * treats undeclared capability.\n */\nexport function workBuddyThinkingLevelMap(info: WorkBuddyModelInfo): WorkBuddyThinkingLevelMap | undefined {\n  const supported = info.reasoning?.supportedEfforts?.filter((effort): effort is WorkBuddyThinkingLevel =>\n    (THINKING_LEVELS as readonly string[]).includes(effort),\n  )\n  if (supported === undefined || supported.length === 0) return undefined\n  const map: WorkBuddyThinkingLevelMap = Object.fromEntries(\n    THINKING_LEVELS.map(level => [level, supported.includes(level) ? level : null]),\n  )\n  if (info.reasoning?.canDisableThinking === true && !offRefused(info.id)) map.off = 'off'\n  else map.off = null\n  return map\n}\n\n/**\n * Build one pi-ai model descriptor pointing at the loopback shim.\n *\n * `identity` — not the route id — is what `provider` carries; see\n * {@link WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY} for why the two must differ. The route the\n * model is REACHED by is the registry key the adapter serves, while this\n * stamped field is what pi-ai resolves the provider BY (issue #75) — so every\n * key in {@link modelIdentityKeysOf} must be a key this route registers.\n */\nfunction toPiModel(\n  info: WorkBuddyModelInfo,\n  baseUrl: string,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n): Model<Api> {\n  const thinkingLevelMap = workBuddyThinkingLevelMap(info)\n  return {\n    id: info.id,\n    name: workBuddyDisplayName(info),\n    api: 'openai-completions',\n    provider: identity,\n    baseUrl,\n    input: workBuddyModelInput(info),\n    cost: NO_COST,\n    contextWindow: info.contextWindow,\n    maxTokens: info.maxTokens,\n    reasoning: thinkingLevelMap !== undefined,\n    ...thinkingLevelMap === undefined ? {} : { thinkingLevelMap },\n    compat: { supportsReasoningEffort: thinkingLevelMap !== undefined },\n  } as unknown as Model<Api>\n}\n\n/**\n * Rewrite a pi-ai 0.87-shaped transcript into the 0.85 shape this plugin's\n * pi-ai understands — prompt AND tools.\n *\n * WHY THIS EXISTS (issue #24). pi-ai 0.87's `normalizeContext` moves the system\n * prompt INTO `messages` as `{ role: 'system', content: '<string>' }`. pi-ai\n * 0.85's own `Message` union has no `system` variant\n * (`UserMessage | AssistantMessage | ToolResultMessage`), so its\n * `estimateMessageTokens` has no branch for one: `for (const block of\n * message.content)` iterates the CONTENT STRING character by character, `block`\n * is a single character, and `block.name.length` throws\n * `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'length')`.\n *\n * That crash happens inside the library, in `buildBaseOptions ->\n * clampMaxTokensToContext -> estimateContextTokens`, i.e. BEFORE any request is\n * built — so every model fails instantly and no upstream traffic is sent. A\n * host running 0.87 hands us the normalized transcript while our own provider\n * is 0.85, and the plugin cannot patch the library. What it CAN do is not hand\n * a 0.87 transcript to a 0.85 API object.\n *\n * The 0.87 transcript carries MORE than the prompt on that leading system\n * message: `toolsAdded` (and `toolsRemoved`) are the ONLY place the tool\n * declarations live — `normalizeContext` deleted the top-level `tools` field,\n * and 0.85's api reads tools ONLY from `context.tools`. So folding the text\n * without promoting the tool state would trade issue #24's loud crash for\n * issue #26's silent one: models that can never emit a `toolCall` because the\n * request never declared any tools. The fold here promotes both, replaying\n * every system message's `toolsRemoved`/`toolsAdded` in order — exactly the\n * merge pi-ai 0.87's own `getCurrentTools` performs — and leaves an existing\n * top-level `tools` array untouched.\n *\n * Deliberately SHAPE-based within the legacy branch, not version-based: it\n * asks \"does this context carry a system message inside `messages`?\" rather\n * than \"which pi-ai version is loaded?\". A version check would be wrong the\n * moment either side moves. Which BRANCH runs at all IS generation-gated —\n * see {@link withLegacyContext}: a modern (0.87+) api consumes this very\n * transcript natively, and folding for it would destroy its only carrier of\n * prompt and tools (issues #25/#26).\n *\n * Returns the SAME object when there is nothing to adapt — in particular when\n * `systemPrompt` is already set (the native 0.85 shape), so the ordinary path\n * is byte-for-byte untouched.\n *\n * Two guards beyond the 0.87 fold, both about a request reaching the upstream\n * with NO system message — which the international gateway refuses outright\n * (business code 11128) while the domestic one tolerates:\n *\n * - an EMPTY `systemPrompt` is treated as absent rather than \"already set\".\n *   pi-ai emits the prompt under `if (context.systemPrompt)`, so an empty one\n *   produces no system message at all and pi-ai demotes any `system` entry still\n *   in `messages` to `user`. Falling through here lets a real system message\n *   that IS present in `messages` be folded up and preserved.\n * - when nothing supplies a prompt, {@link WORKBUDDY_FALLBACK_SYSTEM_PROMPT} is\n *   used, because a placeholder beats a guaranteed 400. A system message whose\n *   text is empty but which carries `toolsAdded` still folds its TOOLS up; the\n *   placeholder for the missing prompt then comes from `ensureSystemHead` at\n *   the wire (the last of the three layers).\n *\n * LEGACY-ONLY. This is the inverse direction from {@link foldModernContext}:\n * it produces the FLAT `{systemPrompt, tools, messages}` shape that ONLY\n * pi-ai 0.85 reads. Never hand its result to a 1.x api — 1.x reads tools from\n * `messages[].toolsAdded` and would see none (issues #25/#26). Type note: the\n * parameter is the 0.85 `Context`; pi-ai 1.1.0 types the api's second parameter\n * as `TranscriptContext`, so this function cannot be called with 1.x typings —\n * that is intentional, and it is why the modern branch below does not touch it.\n */\nexport function adaptLegacyPiAiContext(context: Context): Context {\n  if (context === null || typeof context !== 'object') return context\n  // Truthiness, not `!== undefined`: see the empty-prompt guard above.\n  if (context.systemPrompt) return context\n  const messages = Array.isArray(context.messages) ? context.messages : undefined\n  if (messages === undefined) return context\n\n  const systemTexts: string[] = []\n  const rest: Message[] = []\n  const toolState = new Map<string, NonNullable<Context['tools']>[number]>()\n  let sawSystemMessage = false\n  for (const message of messages) {\n    const role = (message as { role?: unknown } | null)?.role\n    if (role !== 'system') {\n      rest.push(message)\n      continue\n    }\n    sawSystemMessage = true\n    const text = systemTextOf(message)\n    if (text !== '') systemTexts.push(text)\n    replayToolState(message, toolState)\n  }\n\n  const head = rest[0] as { role?: unknown } | undefined\n  const needsFallback = !sawSystemMessage\n    && head !== undefined\n    && head.role !== 'system'\n  if (!sawSystemMessage && !needsFallback) return context\n\n  // Dropped from `messages` even when no text could be extracted: leaving it\n  // there is precisely what crashes 0.85, and a system entry with no text has\n  // nothing to lose. `systemPrompt` stays ABSENT rather than '' so a provider\n  // never receives an empty system message.\n  const systemPrompt = systemTexts.length > 0\n    ? systemTexts.join('\\n\\n')\n    : sawSystemMessage ? undefined : WORKBUDDY_FALLBACK_SYSTEM_PROMPT\n\n  // Only a context with NO top-level tools gets the promoted set: when the\n  // caller supplied one, it is authoritative for their shape.\n  const tools = Array.isArray(context.tools)\n    ? undefined\n    : [...toolState.values()]\n\n  return {\n    ...context,\n    ...systemPrompt === undefined ? {} : { systemPrompt },\n    ...tools !== undefined && tools.length > 0 ? { tools } : {},\n    messages: rest,\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Replay one 0.87 system message's tool deltas into `state`, the same merge\n * pi-ai 0.87's `getCurrentTools` performs: removals first, then additions,\n * keyed by name so a mid-conversation redeclaration replaces the original.\n */\nfunction replayToolState(message: Message, state: Map<string, NonNullable<Context['tools']>[number]>): void {\n  const removed = (message as { toolsRemoved?: unknown }).toolsRemoved\n  if (Array.isArray(removed)) {\n    for (const tool of removed) {\n      const name = (tool as { name?: unknown } | null)?.name\n      if (typeof name === 'string') state.delete(name)\n    }\n  }\n  const added = (message as { toolsAdded?: unknown }).toolsAdded\n  if (Array.isArray(added)) {\n    for (const tool of added) {\n      const name = (tool as { name?: unknown } | null)?.name\n      if (typeof name === 'string') state.set(name, tool as NonNullable<Context['tools']>[number])\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** The text of one system message, whether it is a string or text blocks. */\nfunction systemTextOf(message: Message): string {\n  const content = (message as { content?: unknown }).content\n  if (typeof content === 'string') return content\n  if (!Array.isArray(content)) return ''\n  const texts: string[] = []\n  for (const block of content) {\n    if (typeof block !== 'object' || block === null) continue\n    const candidate = block as { type?: unknown, text?: unknown }\n    if (candidate.type === 'text' && typeof candidate.text === 'string') texts.push(candidate.text)\n  }\n  return texts.join('\\n\\n')\n}\n\n/**\n * Fold a RAW context into the normalized transcript the modern generation\n * consumes: a leading `system` message carrying the prompt AND the tool\n * declarations in its `toolsAdded` field.\n *\n * WHY THIS IS NEEDED AT ALL (the 0.2.1-rc.1 regression). This used to be\n * unnecessary: the modern branch returned pi-ai's api UNWRAPPED, on the belief\n * that the modern generation normalized for itself. It does not.\n * `dsh-llm-pi-ai` — both the 0.1.7 and the 0.2.1 lines — hands every provider a\n * RAW envelope (`piContext()` builds `{systemPrompt, messages, tools}` and\n * never calls `normalizeContext`), while:\n *\n *   - pi-ai 0.85.x READS that raw shape (its request builder consults\n *     `context.systemPrompt` / `context.tools` directly), and\n *   - pi-ai 1.x READS ONLY `context.messages` (it resolves the transcript and\n *     collapses system messages; the top-level fields are consulted nowhere).\n *\n * On pi-ai 1.x a pass-through request therefore went upstream with NO system\n * prompt and NO tool declarations, silently: the model stopped calling tools,\n * and the domestic gateway tolerated the prompt-less body. Same failure class\n * as issues #24/#25/#26, reached from the opposite side — 4.4.2 fixed \"1.x\n * misjudged as legacy and folded\"; this fixes \"1.x judged modern and NOT\n * folded, though nothing had normalized\".\n *\n * Built inline rather than by importing pi-ai's `normalizeContext`, which does\n * not exist in 0.85.x: a static import would be `undefined` there, and this\n * module deliberately never imports pi-ai statically (see `pi-ai-runtime.ts` —\n * that doctrine is what lets the standalone `bin` run in a profile where the\n * peer is never installed). The folded shape matches what `normalizeContext`\n * produces for the same input: `{role:'system', content, toolsAdded, timestamp:0}`.\n *\n * The top-level `systemPrompt`/`tools` fields are DROPPED, exactly as\n * `normalizeContext` drops them, so a raw transcript and an already-normalized\n * one converge on the same object.\n */\nfunction foldModernContext(context: Context): Context {\n  const prompt = context.systemPrompt\n  const tools = context.tools\n  const hasPrompt = prompt !== undefined && prompt !== ''\n  const hasTools = Array.isArray(tools) && tools.length > 0\n  // Nothing to fold: an already-normalized transcript (its system message is in\n  // `messages` already). Folding again would prepend a SECOND, empty one.\n  if (!hasPrompt && !hasTools) return context\n  const system = {\n    role: 'system',\n    content: prompt ?? '',\n    ...(hasTools ? { toolsAdded: tools } : {}),\n    timestamp: 0,\n  } as unknown as Message\n  const { systemPrompt: _prompt, tools: _tools, ...rest } = context as Context & {\n    systemPrompt?: string\n    tools?: Context['tools']\n  }\n  return { ...rest, messages: [system, ...context.messages] } as Context\n}\n\n/**\n * Wrap one pi-ai API module so both stream entry points receive a context this\n * generation can actually consume.\n *\n * The gate is the plugin's own module, resolved via {@link piAiRuntimeInfo}\n * (manifest version of the copy this plugin resolves — no pi-ai import):\n *\n * - `legacy` (0.85) wants the flat `{systemPrompt, tools, messages}` shape, so\n *   a normalized transcript is UN-folded by {@link adaptLegacyPiAiContext}.\n * - `modern` (0.87+, incl. 1.x) wants the normalized transcript, so a raw\n *   envelope is FOLDED by {@link foldModernContext}. Passing it through\n *   unnormalized is what silently deleted the prompt and the tools on pi-ai\n *   1.x — the regression this branch now prevents.\n *\n * Both branches are idempotent, so a future seam that starts normalizing on its\n * own side changes nothing here.\n *\n * The deferred-fetch entry points take no context and are passed through as-is\n * in both branches.\n */\n/**\n * Present an adapted context as the type the api's parameter currently expects.\n *\n * pi-ai 1.x BRANDS its provider-facing context (`TranscriptContext`, a\n * `unique symbol` field) precisely so a raw `Context` cannot reach provider\n * code by accident; the brand's stated sole producer is `normalizeContext()`.\n * On a 1.x typings surface BOTH branches below receive that branded type, so\n * merely returning a plain `Context` fails `tsc` with TS2345 — an error that\n * only appears when the peer TYPES are 1.x, which is why the 0.85 checkout\n * never saw it.\n *\n * The cast is a deliberate, documented assertion of exactly what the brand\n * protects, and it is true after the call site has done the work:\n *\n * - modern: {@link foldModernContext} has already performed\n *   `normalizeContext`'s job — prompt and tools live on the leading system\n *   message, top-level fields gone;\n * - legacy: {@link adaptLegacyPiAiContext} produced the FLAT 0.85 shape, which\n *   is only ever observed on a 0.85 checkout, where this type is a plain\n *   `Context` and the cast is an upcast.\n *\n * Do NOT \"fix\" this by importing `normalizeContext`: it does not exist in\n * 0.85, so that import would be `undefined` at runtime on the very hosts this\n * code must keep working — the same reason `pi-ai-runtime.ts` documents why\n * this module never imports pi-ai statically.\n */\nfunction transcript<T>(context: Context): T {\n  return context as unknown as T\n}\n\n/**\n * Rewrite every historical assistant message to the identity this adapter stamps.\n *\n * ISSUE #102. pi-ai replays a thinking block as `thinking` only when the message\n * that produced it names the same `provider` / `api` / `model` as the request in\n * flight (`utils/transform-messages.js`, the `isSameModel` branch); everything\n * else is flattened to `{type:'text', text: thinking}`.\n *\n * The durable transcript the HOST hands back does not carry the identity. The\n * agent loop writes `source.provider = request.provider`, and `request.provider`\n * is the ROUTE id (`workbuddy` / `workbuddy-global`), while this adapter stamps\n * `provider: WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY` on the model. So:\n *\n *   - CN sessions survive by COINCIDENCE: its route id happens to equal the\n *     identity (`workbuddy` === `workbuddy`).\n *   - GLOBAL sessions never match (`workbuddy-global` !== `workbuddy`), so every\n *     historical thinking block is downgraded on EVERY turn — not only when the\n *     user switches regions, which is what #59's write-up assumed.\n *\n * The model then sees a context in which all of its own past reasoning was\n * plain assistant text, and writes its new reasoning into `text` to match. That\n * is the reported symptom: reasoning collapses to a character or two while the\n * answer channel fills with working notes.\n *\n * WHY REWRITING IS SAFE. Every message here was produced by THIS plugin, in THIS\n * region — the transcript never contains another provider's assistant turns\n * (`dsh-llm` splits history by provider before the API sees it). The identity is\n * already the field this adapter stamps on the models it serves, and it resolves\n * as a registry key in both regions (issue #75). Nothing about routing, billing\n * or the destination changes: `baseUrl` comes from the model, not from the\n * message.\n *\n * `provider` alone is NOT enough, because the HOST drops the message to\n * `api: 'dsh-foreign'` first. `dsh-llm-pi-ai`'s `replayedAssistant` rejects a\n * replay whose recorded provider differs from `source.provider` and falls back\n * to `foreignAssistant()`, which stamps `api: 'dsh-foreign'` and takes\n * `provider` from `source.provider` — the ROUTE id. That is why the provider\n * mismatch is not merely cosmetic: it also costs the message its native api, and\n * `isSameModel` then fails on the SECOND comparison even after `provider` is\n * repaired. Measured on a real 0.2.1-rc.2 global session, aligning `provider`\n * only still flattened a 1514-character thinking block into 1514 characters of\n * text; aligning both restored `thinking`.\n *\n * So `api` is repaired too, but ONLY from the one value that proves it is a\n * host fallback and not this plugin's own wire format: `dsh-foreign`. Any other\n * api is left exactly as it is.\n *\n * WHY THAT IS NOT FORGING A SIGNATURE. A `foreignAssistant()` message carries\n * thinking blocks with NO `thinkingSignature` (`dsh-llm-pi-ai` builds them as\n * `{type:'thinking', thinking}`), and pi-ai's request builder only emits\n * `reasoning_content` when a signature exists\n * (`api/openai-completions.js`, the `if (signature && isOpenAICompletionsReasoningField(...))`\n * branch). The block is therefore OMITTED from the request, never replayed as\n * signed reasoning — the same thing that happens today, minus the visible text.\n * The message's own `api` field is not read by the request builder at all\n * (grep: only `model.api` is), so this changes no wire shape.\n *\n * A genuinely different model still degrades, because `isSameModel` requires the\n * model id to match and this function deliberately does not rewrite it: the\n * message really was produced by another model. {@link dropForeignThinking}\n * covers that leg separately, through its `requestModelId` argument.\n *\n * Returns the same object when nothing changed, so the common case (an\n * already-normalized transcript) allocates nothing.\n */\nexport function alignAssistantIdentities(\n  context: Context,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  ownedRoutes: readonly string[] = [WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER, WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER],\n): Context {\n  const messages = context.messages\n  if (!Array.isArray(messages) || messages.length === 0) return context\n  // ISSUE #111: THE MESSAGE MUST BELONG TO THIS PLUGIN FIRST. The docblock\n  // above used to claim `dsh-llm` splits history by provider, so a transcript\n  // reaching this adapter could only contain this plugin's own turns. That is\n  // NOT what the host does: `forAdapter()` (dsh-llm/lib/index.js:2151) only\n  // STRIPS `replayState` from a message whose `source.provider` belongs to\n  // another adapter — it keeps the message in the list. So a session that\n  // switched providers (the user goes from DeepSeek official to WorkBuddy, or\n  // back) hands this adapter assistant turns that this plugin never produced,\n  // and the old code rewrote THEIR `provider` to `workbuddy` and their `api`\n  // to `openai-completions`.\n  //\n  // That is worse than cosmetic. It relabels a foreign turn as this plugin's\n  // own model output, so pi-ai's `isSameModel` compares it against the request\n  // and can replay its reasoning as if this model had produced it. It also\n  // makes the transcript claim a provenance that is false — the host's own\n  // `forAdapter` went out of its way to clear `replayState` precisely so a\n  // foreign turn would NOT be replayed, and rewriting the identity handed that\n  // turn back through the door `forAdapter` had just closed.\n  //\n  // So the rewrite is scoped to the routes this bundle owns. A message from any\n  // other provider is left EXACTLY as the host built it: its `api` stays\n  // `dsh-foreign`, which is the host's honest marker for \"not this adapter's\n  // wire format\", and its `provider` stays whatever that adapter registered.\n  const owned = new Set(ownedRoutes)\n  let changed = false\n  const next = messages.map(message => {\n    if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return message\n    const record = message as unknown as Record<string, unknown>\n    if (record['role'] !== 'assistant') return message\n    const provider = record['provider']\n    // A foreign turn keeps its own identity. `identity` is accepted here too\n    // because the identity IS this plugin's stamp: a message already carrying\n    // it is ours by definition (that is the CN case, where route and identity\n    // are the same string).\n    if (typeof provider !== 'string' || !owned.has(provider) && provider !== identity) return message\n    const providerAligned = provider === identity\n    // Only the host's own fallback marker is repaired; every other api is the\n    // shape this plugin really sends and must be preserved verbatim.\n    const apiAligned = record['api'] !== HOST_FOREIGN_API\n    if (providerAligned && apiAligned) return message\n    changed = true\n    const aligned = { ...record }\n    if (!providerAligned) aligned['provider'] = identity\n    if (!apiAligned) aligned['api'] = 'openai-completions'\n    return aligned as unknown as typeof message\n  })\n  return changed ? { ...context, messages: next } : context\n}\n\n/**\n * One foreign thinking block that pi-ai would publish as PROSE, and the reason it\n * would (issue #147).\n *\n * `variant` is not cosmetic: the two are handled by the same repair but they\n * mean different things, and the card explains them differently.\n *\n * - `flattened`: `stopReason` is a normal completion, so `transform-messages`\n *   turns the block into `{type:'text', text: thinking}` — the whole reasoning\n *   body lands in the visible answer.\n * - `dropped`: `stopReason` is `error`/`aborted`, so the entire message is\n *   skipped; nothing reaches the wire and nothing reaches the user. It is\n *   reported anyway, because it still costs context: the model's answer to\n *   \"why did my last turn say nothing\" is this line, and a caller cannot see it\n *   from the transcript.\n */\nexport interface ForeignThinkingBlock {\n  /** Index of the message in the context's `messages`. */\n  messageIndex: number\n  /** Index of the block within that message's `content`. */\n  blockIndex: number\n  /** How many characters of reasoning the block carried. */\n  characters: number\n  /** Whether pi-ai would flatten it into prose or drop the whole message. */\n  variant: 'flattened' | 'dropped'\n}\n\n/** Read `content` as a block array without assuming the shape. */\nfunction blocksOf(message: unknown): unknown[] {\n  if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return []\n  const content = (message as Record<string, unknown>)['content']\n  return Array.isArray(content) ? content : []\n}\n\n/**\n * Whether this assistant message's identity proves a DIFFERENT model produced it.\n *\n * `requestModelId` closes the THIRD leg of pi-ai's own predicate. `isSameModel`\n * is `provider === … && api === … && model === model.id`\n * (`transform-messages.js:68–70`); this function used to compare only the first\n * two. The consequence was a blind spot the size of one whole route: a session\n * that switches MODELS inside one region — `hy3` → `deepseek-v4.1-flash`, both\n * served by this bundle, both stamped `provider: workbuddy` — keeps the provider\n * and api legs equal, so nothing here noticed, while pi-ai flattened every\n * historical thinking block into the answer channel exactly as in issue #147.\n * The user-visible symptom is #147's, with none of its diagnostics.\n *\n * Passing `undefined` keeps the provider/api-only behaviour, which is what the\n * read-only surfaces and the older call sites expect. Production passes the id\n * of the model the request is actually addressed to.\n */\nfunction isForeignAssistant(\n  message: unknown,\n  identity: string,\n  requestModelId?: string,\n): boolean {\n  if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return false\n  const record = message as Record<string, unknown>\n  if (record['role'] !== 'assistant') return false\n  // The host's own fallback marker beats everything: `foreignAssistant()` sets\n  // it precisely to say \"this is not the wire format the current request uses\".\n  if (record['api'] === HOST_FOREIGN_API) return true\n  const provider = record['provider']\n  if (typeof provider !== 'string' || provider !== identity\n    && provider !== WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER && provider !== WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER) return true\n  // Model leg. A message this bundle produced still degrades when the request\n  // names a different model id, because that is what `isSameModel` compares.\n  // `undefined` on either side means \"cannot compare\": stay silent rather than\n  // report a block pi-ai would have kept.\n  if (requestModelId === undefined) return false\n  const model = record['model']\n  return typeof model === 'string' && model !== requestModelId\n}\n\n/**\n * Whether this assistant turn came from a DIFFERENT PROVIDER/route, rather than\n * from a different model on one of this bundle's own routes.\n *\n * WHY THIS DISTINCTION EXISTS (issue #163's live scene). A session switched\n * from `dsh-opencode-go` to a WorkBuddy route mid-turn. The host rebuilt the\n * other provider's assistant turns as `dsh-foreign`, and pi-ai rewrote their\n * unsigned `thinking` blocks into visible `text` — 14,410 / 4,225 / 5,138\n * characters in the measured scene. WorkBuddy's gateway scans every assistant\n * message and refused the turn with business code 11128; all three in-place\n * rescue rungs and three auto-`继续` attempts failed because the bytes never\n * changed.\n *\n * A cross-provider thinking block is different from a same-route model switch:\n * it carries no `thinkingSignature` (the host builds `foreignAssistant()`\n * without one), so it could never be replayed as reasoning. Its only possible\n * contribution to the wire is the prose pi-ai manufactures from it. Dropping it\n * is therefore a correctness repair, not a preference — and it must happen\n * even when the user never opted into {@link dropForeignThinking}.\n *\n * Same-route model switches (`hy3` → `deepseek-v4.1-flash`) stay behind the\n * explicit switch, because those turns WERE produced by this bundle and their\n * reasoning may be worth keeping in the transcript for the user to read.\n */\nfunction isCrossProviderAssistant(\n  message: unknown,\n  identity: string,\n  ownedRoutes: readonly string[],\n): boolean {\n  if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) return false\n  const record = message as Record<string, unknown>\n  if (record['role'] !== 'assistant') return false\n  // The host's fallback marker is the strongest signal: `foreignAssistant()`\n  // sets it for a turn this adapter did not produce. After\n  // `alignAssistantIdentities`, a turn this bundle owns has had the marker\n  // repaired away, so a remaining marker means genuinely foreign.\n  if (record['api'] === HOST_FOREIGN_API) return true\n  const provider = record['provider']\n  if (typeof provider !== 'string') return true\n  return provider !== identity && !ownedRoutes.includes(provider)\n}\n\n/**\n * Every foreign thinking block pi-ai would turn into visible prose (issue #147).\n *\n * THE DEFECT. pi-ai keeps a historical `thinking` block only while\n * `isSameModel` holds — provider AND api AND model id all equal to the request\n * (`transform-messages.js:68`). For everything else it does NOT drop the block:\n * `:87–90` rewrites it to `{type:'text', text: thinking}`, which is the answer\n * channel. A session that moved between providers therefore arrives with its\n * other provider's working notes already spelled as prose, and the model reads\n * that shape and copies it.\n *\n * Issue #147 measured the consequence on a real 80-step turn: 56 assistant\n * messages carried an answer while their own `reasoning` was ≤ 1 character,\n * 20 of them held English working notes totalling 58,960 characters, and CJK\n * fell to 1.37% of the visible text where the preceding six turns had been\n * entirely Chinese. That is not a formatting complaint — it also pushed the\n * session from 696,966 to 758,005 total tokens against an 800,000 compression\n * threshold.\n *\n * WHY THIS IS READ-ONLY. Nothing here rewrites the transcript:\n * {@link dropForeignThinking} does, and the split keeps the DIAGNOSIS available\n * to surfaces that must not change bytes (the card, the reply notice).\n *\n * @param context - a pi-ai context, before or after `alignAssistantIdentities`.\n * @param identity - the identity this adapter stamps on its own models.\n * @param requestModelId - model id the request is addressed to, so the model\n *   leg of pi-ai's predicate is covered too (see {@link isForeignAssistant}).\n *   Omitted, only the provider/api legs are judged.\n * @returns one entry per affected block, in transcript order.\n */\nexport function foreignThinkingOf(\n  context: Context,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  requestModelId?: string,\n): ForeignThinkingBlock[] {\n  const messages = (context as { messages?: unknown }).messages\n  if (!Array.isArray(messages)) return []\n  const found: ForeignThinkingBlock[] = []\n  for (const [messageIndex, message] of messages.entries()) {\n    if (!isForeignAssistant(message, identity, requestModelId)) continue\n    const record = message as Record<string, unknown>\n    const stopReason = record['stopReason']\n    // Mirrors `transform-messages.js`: an errored or aborted turn is skipped\n    // wholesale, so its blocks would not have surfaced either way.\n    const variant = stopReason === 'error' || stopReason === 'aborted' ? 'dropped' : 'flattened'\n    for (const [blockIndex, block] of blocksOf(message).entries()) {\n      if (typeof block !== 'object' || block === null) continue\n      const fields = block as Record<string, unknown>\n      if (fields['type'] !== 'thinking') continue\n      // A REDACTED block is never shown as prose: pi-ai returns `[]` for it\n      // (`:75–77`), so it is neither a leak nor a context cost worth naming.\n      if (fields['redacted'] === true) continue\n      const thinking = fields['thinking']\n      const characters = typeof thinking === 'string' ? thinking.length : 0\n      // Empty reasoning is dropped before any of the branches that matter\n      // (`:83–84`), so reporting it would be a false positive.\n      if (characters === 0) continue\n      found.push({ messageIndex, blockIndex, characters, variant })\n    }\n  }\n  return found\n}\n\n/**\n * The subset of {@link foreignThinkingOf} that is safe to repair without asking.\n *\n * This is the predicate the adapter uses when `dropForeignThinking` is OFF: a\n * turn from another provider/route is dropped unconditionally, because its\n * unsigned thinking block can never be replayed and its flattened prose is what\n * the WorkBuddy gateway refuses (issue #163's provider-switch scene). A turn\n * from this bundle under a different model id is NOT included — that one is\n * still the explicit switch's business.\n *\n * Runs after `alignAssistantIdentities`, so a global-region turn this bundle\n * owns has already had its `dsh-foreign` marker repaired away and is not\n * misclassified.\n */\nexport function crossProviderThinkingOf(\n  context: Context,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  ownedRoutes: readonly string[] = [WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER, WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER],\n): ForeignThinkingBlock[] {\n  const messages = (context as { messages?: unknown }).messages\n  if (!Array.isArray(messages)) return []\n  return foreignThinkingOf(context, identity).filter(block =>\n    isCrossProviderAssistant(messages[block.messageIndex], identity, ownedRoutes))\n}\n\n/**\n * Remove the foreign thinking blocks {@link foreignThinkingOf} finds.\n *\n * WHY REMOVAL IS THE HONEST REPAIR. The alternatives are both worse:\n *\n *   - LEAVE IT: pi-ai publishes the other provider's private working notes as\n *     this turn's answer (the #147 defect), so the user reads reasoning instead\n *     of a reply and the model imitates that shape forever after.\n *   - KEEP IT AS THINKING: impossible from here. `isSameModel` is pi-ai's own\n *     predicate over fields this plugin does not control (`model.provider` /\n *     `model.api` / `model.id` are compared against the MESSAGE's), and the\n *     only way to satisfy it is to claim the foreign turn as this model's own\n *     output — which is exactly the provenance lie issue #111 removed. These\n *     blocks carry no `thinkingSignature` (the host builds them that way), so\n *     they could not be replayed as signed reasoning even if claimed.\n *\n * Dropping is what the request builder already does with them in every other\n * branch: an unsigned foreign thinking block is never sent as\n * `reasoning_content`, so its only possible contribution to the wire was the\n * flattened prose this function removes. Every other block — text, tool calls —\n * is left exactly where it is, and so is every message's identity.\n *\n * Idempotent: a second pass finds nothing to remove. Returns the SAME context\n * object when nothing changed, so the common case allocates nothing.\n *\n * @param context - a pi-ai context.\n * @param identity - the identity this adapter stamps on its own models.\n * @param requestModelId - model id the request is addressed to (see\n *   {@link isForeignAssistant}); omitted, only the provider/api legs are judged.\n * @returns the repaired context, or the input when no block qualified.\n */\nexport function dropForeignThinking(\n  context: Context,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  requestModelId?: string,\n): Context {\n  return dropThinkingBlocks(context, foreignThinkingOf(context, identity, requestModelId))\n}\n\n/** Remove exactly the listed blocks; the one place that edits a transcript. */\nfunction dropThinkingBlocks(context: Context, affected: readonly ForeignThinkingBlock[]): Context {\n  if (affected.length === 0) return context\n  const messages = (context as unknown as { messages: unknown[] }).messages\n  // Group by message first: each message is copied at most once, and a message\n  // whose content becomes empty is left in place (pi-ai drops it anyway; moving\n  // it here would renumber tool-result pairing for no benefit).\n  const byMessage = new Map<number, Set<number>>()\n  for (const block of affected) {\n    const set = byMessage.get(block.messageIndex) ?? new Set<number>()\n    set.add(block.blockIndex)\n    byMessage.set(block.messageIndex, set)\n  }\n  const next = messages.map((message, index) => {\n    const remove = byMessage.get(index)\n    if (remove === undefined) return message\n    const record = message as Record<string, unknown>\n    const content = blocksOf(message).filter((_, blockIndex) => !remove.has(blockIndex))\n    return { ...record, content }\n  })\n  return { ...context, messages: next } as Context\n}\n\n/**\n * How deeply this wrapper repairs the transcript before it reaches pi-ai.\n *\n * Both members are OFF by default, so a config that never mentions them sends\n * byte-for-byte what earlier builds sent (see {@link WorkBuddyAdapterOptions}).\n */\nexport interface TranscriptRepairOptions {\n  /**\n   * Drop SAME-ROUTE foreign thinking blocks pi-ai would publish as prose\n   * (issue #147). `false` keeps the previous behaviour for a model switch\n   * inside one route.\n   *\n   * Cross-provider turns are repaired regardless of this switch (issue #163's\n   * provider-switch scene): their unsigned thinking blocks can never be\n   * replayed, and their flattened prose is what the gateway refuses.\n   */\n  dropForeignThinking?: boolean\n  /**\n   * Told how many blocks that repair removed and where, so a surface can name\n   * the condition the user is actually looking at. Called at most once per\n   * request, and only when something was removed.\n   */\n  onForeignThinking?: (blocks: readonly ForeignThinkingBlock[]) => void\n}\n\nfunction withLegacyContext(\n  api: ProviderStreams,\n  generation: PiAiGeneration,\n  identity: string = WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY,\n  ownedRoutes: readonly string[] = [WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER, WORKBUDDY_GLOBAL_PROVIDER],\n  repair?: TranscriptRepairOptions,\n): ProviderStreams {\n  /** One place so both branches and both entry points agree on the order. */\n  const prepare = (context: Context, requestModelId?: string): Context => {\n    const folded = generation === 'modern' ? foldModernContext(context) : adaptLegacyPiAiContext(context)\n    const aligned = alignAssistantIdentities(folded, identity, ownedRoutes)\n    // ISSUE #147, AFTER alignment on purpose. `foreignThinkingOf` reads the\n    // identity to decide what is foreign, so running it first would classify a\n    // turn this plugin OWNS (route id still unaligned) as foreign and delete its\n    // reasoning — issue #102's regression, from the other direction.\n    //\n    // ISSUE #163: a CROSS-PROVIDER turn is repaired even when the switch is\n    // off. Its thinking block has no signature and can never be replayed, and\n    // pi-ai would otherwise flatten it into assistant prose — which is the\n    // content the WorkBuddy gateway refused in the measured provider-switch\n    // scene. The switch only widens this to a same-route model switch, whose\n    // turn this bundle did produce.\n    //\n    // `requestModelId` is pi-ai's third predicate leg. Without it a switch\n    // BETWEEN MODELS inside one region (`hy3` → `deepseek-v4.1-flash`, both on\n    // this route) degraded exactly like a cross-provider switch, while this\n    // repair saw nothing to do: provider and api both still matched.\n    const affected = repair?.dropForeignThinking === true\n      ? foreignThinkingOf(aligned, identity, requestModelId)\n      : crossProviderThinkingOf(aligned, identity, ownedRoutes)\n    if (affected.length === 0) return aligned\n    try {\n      repair?.onForeignThinking?.(affected)\n    } catch {\n      // A diagnostic must never fail a chat request.\n    }\n    return dropThinkingBlocks(aligned, affected)\n  }\n  if (generation === 'modern') {\n    return {\n      ...api,\n      stream: (model, context, options) =>\n        api.stream(model, transcript<Parameters<typeof api.stream>[1]>(prepare(context, model.id)), options),\n      streamSimple: (model, context, options) =>\n        api.streamSimple(\n          model,\n          transcript<Parameters<typeof api.streamSimple>[1]>(prepare(context, model.id)),\n          options,\n        ),\n    }\n  }\n  return {\n    ...api,\n    stream: (model, context, options) =>\n      api.stream(model, transcript<Parameters<typeof api.stream>[1]>(prepare(context, model.id)), options),\n    streamSimple: (model, context, options) =>\n      api.streamSimple(\n        model,\n        transcript<Parameters<typeof api.streamSimple>[1]>(prepare(context, model.id)),\n        options,\n      ),\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Layer the host-side context-overflow rescue onto a provider's streams.\n *\n * pi-ai reports a provider failure as an `error` event rather than a throw, so\n * the rescue runs while the failing assistant message is still in hand: history\n * is folded before the event continues on its way, and the host's retry then\n * rebuilds its request against a smaller surface. Nothing is swallowed — the\n * error event is forwarded unchanged, because deciding whether to retry is the\n * host's policy, not this transport's.\n *\n * Both gates are deliberate. Without `sessionId` there is no session to fold,\n * and without a declared `contextWindow` there is no budget to fold against;\n * either absence leaves the stream untouched.\n */\nfunction withContextRescue(\n  api: ProviderStreams,\n  resolve: (() => ContextRescue | undefined) | undefined,\n): ProviderStreams {\n  if (resolve === undefined) return api\n\n  // Both parameters stay generic so this wrapper serves whichever pi-ai\n  // generation is installed: 1.x replaced the flat `Context` with a branded\n  // `TranscriptContext`, and naming either type here would pin the wrapper to\n  // one generation and break the other's typecheck.\n  const wrap = <ContextShape, Options>(\n    inner: (model: Model<Api>, context: ContextShape, options?: Options) => AssistantMessageEventStream,\n  ) =>\n    (model: Model<Api>, context: ContextShape, options?: Options): AssistantMessageEventStream => {\n      const source = inner(model, context, options)\n      const rescue = resolve()\n      // 0.85 and 1.x both carry the session id here under the same name; the\n      // structural read keeps the wrapper independent of the options type.\n      const sessionId = (options as { sessionId?: string } | undefined)?.sessionId\n      const window = model.contextWindow\n      if (rescue === undefined || sessionId === undefined || window === undefined) return source\n\n      const out = createAssistantMessageEventStream()\n      let final: AssistantMessage | undefined\n      void (async () => {\n        try {\n          for await (const event of source) {\n            if (event.type === 'done') final = event.message\n            if (event.type === 'error') final = event.error\n            // Fold before the consumer settles: the error still reaches it, but\n            // the surface is already smaller when the host retries.\n            if (event.type === 'error' && isContextOverflow(event.error)) {\n              await rescue.rescue(sessionId, window)\n            }\n            out.push(event)\n          }\n          out.end(final)\n        } catch {\n          // Unreachable in practice: pi-ai converts every provider failure into\n          // an `error` event. Close the stream rather than leave a consumer\n          // waiting on an iterator that will never advance.\n          out.end(final)\n        }\n      })()\n      return out\n    }\n\n  return { ...api, stream: wrap(api.stream), streamSimple: wrap(api.streamSimple) }\n}\n\n/**\n * The tier {@link EffortNormalizingAdapter} pins a model to when the model\n * carries no reasoning metadata of its own.\n *\n * `off` is the ONE tier pi-ai offers for such a model (`getSupportedThinkingLevels`\n * returns `['off']` whenever `model.reasoning` is false), and the host's own\n * translation of it is \"send no reasoning field at all\" (`profileOptions` turns\n * `off` into `enabledReasoning = undefined`). So pinning it neither invents a\n * capability nor changes one byte of the upstream request body.\n */\nconst NO_REASONING_EFFORT = ReasoningEffortId('off')\n\n/** What the host is told a reasoning-less model supports: exactly that tier. */\nconst NO_REASONING_EFFORTS: LlmModelReasoningInfo = {\n  efforts: [{ id: NO_REASONING_EFFORT, name: 'Off' }],\n}\n\n/**\n * pi-ai adapter that keeps a model WITHOUT reasoning metadata dispatchable\n * (issue #90, the `UNSUPPORTED_REASONING_EFFORT` half).\n *\n * THE FAILURE. `profileFor` pins one default tier per ROUTE\n * (`reasoning: WORKBUDDY_DEFAULT_EFFORT`, issue #83), and `dsh-llm-pi-ai`\n * resolves every request as `options.reasoningEffort ?? profile.reasoning`. For\n * a model whose catalog entry carries no `reasoning` block at all — the live CN\n * catalog has six of them (`default`, `glm-5.0`, `glm-4.7`, `glm-4.6`,\n * `hunyuan-chat`, `hunyuan-image-alpha`), the live global one two\n * (`default-model`, `deep-model`), and EVERY entry of both static fallback lists\n * — the inherited `high` is not a tier that model has. (`default-model` is the\n * international `auto` router and is not offered as a row since 5.0.1; the\n * measurement is kept verbatim because the defect it documents was observed\n * against the full roster.) `resolveReasoningLevel`\n * then throws `UNSUPPORTED_REASONING_EFFORT` before the request is even built,\n * so the model is unusable locally: no wire call, no upstream error, nothing to\n * retry. Introduced by v4.11.0, which made the profile default explicit; before\n * it the default was absent and pi-ai clamped instead of throwing.\n *\n * TWO GATES, BOTH CLOSED. The host checks the adapter's own model metadata\n * first: `resolveCallWithInfo` rejects ANY explicitly requested tier on a model\n * whose `reasoning` is absent, and `reasoningInfo` omits the field exactly for\n * models without metadata — so even `off` was refused. Then `PiAiAdapter.streamWithSnapshot`\n * rejects the route default it inherited. A fix at either seam alone still\n * fails at the other.\n *\n * THE FIX. Advertise the one tier pi-ai will actually accept for such a model\n * (`off`) and normalize the request to it at the dispatch seam, so\n * `resolveReasoningLevel` sees a supported tier and `profileOptions` sends no\n * reasoning field. Nothing about the wire body changes — pi-ai never emits\n * `reasoning_effort` for a model whose descriptor has `reasoning: false`.\n *\n * WHAT STAYS STRICT. A tier the caller EXPLICITLY asks for on a model that\n * cannot do it is still rejected, by the host, with an accurate message. That\n * is the pre-v4.11.0 contract too, and it is not this bug: the bug is a request\n * that named no tier being killed by the route's own default.\n */\nclass EffortNormalizingAdapter extends PiAiAdapter {\n  /**\n   * Whether one exact model id currently carries reasoning metadata. Read live\n   * on every call because the catalog is refreshed behind the adapter\n   * (`invalidate`), and a model that gains metadata mid-session must stop being\n   * normalized at once.\n   */\n  private readonly effortCapable: (model: string) => boolean\n\n  /**\n   * The tier one exact model should advertise as its default when a caller\n   * names none (issue #179).\n   *\n   * Read LIVE per resolve/prepare call, like {@link effortCapable}: the user can\n   * change the choice mid-session, and `applySelection` re-invalidates the\n   * catalog on every settings change. A captured value would make the picker\n   * show the new default while requests kept the old one.\n   *\n   * Returns `undefined` for a model with no tier vocabulary — the caller then\n   * leaves the advertised `reasoning` block exactly as pi-ai built it, which is\n   * the `off`-only shape `advertiseOffOnly` below depends on.\n   */\n  private readonly defaultEffort: (model: string) => string | undefined\n\n  constructor(\n    config: PiAiAdapterOptions,\n    effortCapable: (model: string) => boolean,\n    defaultEffort: (model: string) => string | undefined = () => undefined,\n  ) {\n    super(config)\n    this.effortCapable = effortCapable\n    this.defaultEffort = defaultEffort\n  }\n\n  override async resolveModel(provider: string, model: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<LlmResolvedModelInfo> {\n    return this.advertiseDefaultEffort(this.advertiseOffOnly(await super.resolveModel(provider, model, signal)))\n  }\n\n  override async prepareCall(provider: string, model: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<PreparedAdapterCall> {\n    const call = await super.prepareCall(provider, model, signal)\n    // Both halves matter: the host validates `model` before it ever calls\n    // `stream`, and `stream` is what actually reaches `resolveReasoningLevel`.\n    return {\n      model: this.advertiseDefaultEffort(this.advertiseOffOnly(call.model)),\n      stream: (options: GenerateOptions): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk> => call.stream(this.pinEffort(options)),\n    }\n  }\n\n  override stream(options: GenerateOptions): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk> {\n    return super.stream(this.pinEffort(options))\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Advertise the per-model default tier (issue #179).\n   *\n   * The host materializes `reasoningEffort` into EVERY request that names none\n   * from this exact field (`LlmRuntime.resolveCallWithInfo`), which is what makes\n   * a saved per-model default real rather than a display preference: without\n   * `defaultEffort` the host leaves the config field empty and pi-ai falls back\n   * to `profile.reasoning` — the one route-wide constant this feature exists to\n   * replace.\n   *\n   * Deliberately a no-op when the model declares no tier: pi-ai's `off`-only\n   * block must keep its EXACT advertised shape, or the host's prepared-call\n   * config check (`callConfigEquals`) starts disagreeing with callers that omit\n   * the field — see `advertiseOffOnly` above.\n   */\n  private advertiseDefaultEffort(info: LlmResolvedModelInfo): LlmResolvedModelInfo {\n    if (info.reasoning === undefined) return info\n    const effort = this.defaultEffort(info.id)\n    if (effort === undefined) return info\n    // Never contradict the tier list: an entry the host would reject is worse\n    // than no default at all (issue #90's `UNSUPPORTED_REASONING_EFFORT`).\n    if (!info.reasoning.efforts.some(level => level.id === effort)) return info\n    if (info.reasoning.defaultEffort === effort) return info\n    return {\n      ...info,\n      reasoning: { ...info.reasoning, defaultEffort: ReasoningEffortId(effort) },\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Give a reasoning-less model the one tier it can dispatch at. No\n   * `defaultEffort` on purpose: the host materializes one into EVERY request\n   * that names no tier, and that would turn the host's own prepared-call config\n   * check (`callConfigEquals`) against callers that omit the field.\n   */\n  private advertiseOffOnly(info: LlmResolvedModelInfo): LlmResolvedModelInfo {\n    return info.reasoning === undefined ? { ...info, reasoning: NO_REASONING_EFFORTS } : info\n  }\n\n  /** Replace an unusable tier with the one pi-ai accepts for this model. */\n  private pinEffort(options: GenerateOptions): GenerateOptions {\n    if (this.effortCapable(options.model)) return options\n    return options.reasoningEffort === NO_REASONING_EFFORT\n      ? options\n      : { ...options, reasoningEffort: NO_REASONING_EFFORT }\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Assemble the adapter. The provider's `getModels` reads the live catalog,\n * and every model's `baseUrl` is re-resolved per read so the shim's\n * ephemeral port applies from the first snapshot after startup.\n */\nexport function createWorkBuddyAdapter(options: WorkBuddyAdapterOptions): WorkBuddyAdapter {\n  const { shim, store, catalog, resolveAttachments } = options\n  const providerId = options.provider ?? WORKBUDDY_PROVIDER\n  const providerName = options.displayName ?? 'WorkBuddy'\n  // The model identity is shared by both regions on purpose — that is the whole\n  // of issue #59's fix, and it is NOT inert (issue #75): `requireProvider` and\n  // `getAuth` resolve the registry BY the stamped `model.provider`. The route\n  // id below is NOT shared: it keys the registry a user selects from, and each\n  // region's route resolves to that region's own shim. So the identity is\n  // published as an EXTRA registry key of the same route (see\n  // `modelIdentityKeysOf`) instead of replacing the route id — the two halves\n  // of #59 and #75 that way stay true at once.\n  const modelIdentity = options.modelIdentity ?? WORKBUDDY_MODEL_IDENTITY\n  // The generation gate: what THIS plugin resolves, not what the host runs —\n  // a nested 0.85 on disk shadows the host's 0.87 and vice versa, and only\n  // the resolved copy decides which context shape the api below can consume.\n  const generation = options.piAiGeneration ?? piAiRuntimeInfo().generation\n  void store\n\n  const buildModels = (): Model<Api>[] => {\n    // The OpenAI SDK pi-ai drives appends `/chat/completions` to baseURL,\n    // so the shim's routes line up with the `/v1` prefix in place.\n    const baseUrl = `${shim.baseUrl()}/v1`\n    return catalog.current().map(info => toPiModel(info, baseUrl, modelIdentity))\n  }\n\n  const base = createProvider({\n    id: providerId,\n    name: providerName,\n    auth: {\n      apiKey: {\n        name: 'WorkBuddy OAuth bearer token',\n        async resolve({ credential }) {\n          const apiKey = credential?.key\n          return apiKey === undefined || apiKey.length === 0\n            ? undefined\n            : { auth: { apiKey }, source: 'WorkBuddy' }\n        },\n      },\n    },\n    models: buildModels(),\n    api: withContextRescue(\n      // BOTH of this bundle's routes are owned by this adapter, so a message\n      // stamped with either is ours to align (issue #111). Anything else in\n      // the transcript belongs to another provider and is left untouched.\n      withLegacyContext(openAICompletionsApi(), generation, modelIdentity, undefined, {\n        // Both read per request: the switch is meant to be flipped WHILE\n        // looking at the bad turn, and a captured boolean would need a reload.\n        get dropForeignThinking() { return options.dropForeignThinking?.() === true },\n        ...options.onForeignThinking === undefined ? {} : { onForeignThinking: options.onForeignThinking },\n      }),\n      options.resolveContextRescue,\n    ),\n  })\n\n  // `getModels` is delegated to a live read (the reuse-catalog pattern from\n  // dsh-llm-pi-ai): stream dispatch still runs through the constructed\n  // provider, while the catalog answer tracks the upstream refresh.\n  const provider: Provider = { ...base, getModels: () => buildModels() }\n\n  // One profile per published registry key. The `piProvider` is what actually\n  // lands in the registry, and `Models.setProvider` keys that map on\n  // `piProvider.id` (`models.js:37` on 0.85.1, `:49` on 1.1.0) — NOT on the\n  // profile's `provider` field — so the identity key needs a provider object\n  // that CLAIMS that id. It is the route's own provider under a second id, so\n  // the models, baseUrl, api and auth the request runs with stay this region's\n  // and nothing about the gateway is duplicated. Sharing the very same object\n  // under both keys would register the route id twice and leave the stamped\n  // identity unresolved, which is exactly issue #75.\n  const profileFor = (key: string): ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile => ({\n    provider: key,\n    displayName: providerName,\n    streamIdleTimeoutMs: WORKBUDDY_STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS,\n    retryPolicy: resolveRetryPolicy(undefined, 'dsh-connect-workbuddy retryPolicy'),\n    configuredMaxTokens: new Map(),\n    // Per-model failures gate every request: `modelOf` throws INVALID_CONFIG\n    // for any id present here. The workbuddy catalog is built from live reads,\n    // so an empty map is the accurate answer — no known-bad model — and the\n    // route must never pre-condemn a model that the catalog later supplies.\n    // (Required by ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile since 0.1.5-rc.1.)\n    modelErrors: new Map(),\n    /**\n     * The ROUTE-WIDE fallback tier, pinned to the SAME value pi-ai's level map\n     * would fill in for an absent one (issue #83).\n     *\n     * `dsh-llm-pi-ai` resolves every request's tier as\n     * `options.reasoningEffort ?? profile.reasoning`, so an absent default here\n     * means \"whatever pi-ai fills in\" — and for a model whose map carried\n     * `off → \"off\"`, that was `\"off\"`, i.e. every compaction summary asked a\n     * model like `deepseek-v4.1-flash` to disable thinking, which it answers with\n     * 400 / 11133. Nothing structurally prevents a level or a catalog snapshot\n     * from disagreeing with the adapter's own map, so the default must never be\n     * that.\n     *\n     * Since issue #179 this is only the LAST resort: `advertiseDefaultEffort`\n     * publishes the user's per-MODEL choice as `defaultEffort`, and the host\n     * materializes that into every request that names none — so a user who\n     * picked `low` for one model gets `low` while every other model keeps its own\n     * upstream default. This constant survives for a model whose catalog entry\n     * declares no default at all, which is the only case that reaches here.\n     */\n    reasoning: WORKBUDDY_DEFAULT_EFFORT,\n    ...REQUEST_IMAGE_BUDGETS,\n    piProvider: key === providerId ? provider : { ...provider, id: key },\n  })\n\n  // Single source for BOTH the initial map and `invalidate`: an `invalidate`\n  // that rebuilt only the route key would silently re-break the identity key,\n  // i.e. issue #75 all over again on the first catalog refresh.\n  const buildProfiles = (): Map<string, ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile> =>\n    new Map(modelIdentityKeysOf(providerId, modelIdentity)\n      .map((key): [string, ResolvedPiAiProviderProfile] => [key, profileFor(key)]))\n\n  // Replacing (not mutating) the map is what `invalidate` uses to force the\n  // adapter's next profiles read to rebuild its snapshot.\n  let profiles = buildProfiles()\n\n  /**\n   * Whether one exact model id currently advertises ANY thinking tier.\n   *\n   * Same predicate `toPiModel` derives the descriptor's `reasoning` flag from,\n   * so the two can never disagree — and it reads the live catalog, so a refresh\n   * that adds (or drops) metadata for a model takes effect on the next request\n   * without an `invalidate` round trip.\n   */\n  const effortCapable = (model: string): boolean =>\n    catalog.current().some(info => info.id === model && workBuddyThinkingLevelMap(info) !== undefined)\n\n  /**\n   * The tier one exact model should run on when a caller names none (#179).\n   *\n   * Resolved against the LIVE catalog and the user's LIVE saved map, exactly\n   * like `effortCapable`: the answer is a function of two things that both\n   * change mid-session, and caching either would let the advertised default and\n   * the dispatched tier drift apart.\n   */\n  const resolveDefaultEffort = (model: string): string | undefined => {\n    const info = catalog.current().find(entry => entry.id === model)\n    if (info === undefined) return undefined\n    return defaultEffortFor(info, options.defaultThinkingEfforts?.() ?? {})\n  }\n\n  const adapter = new EffortNormalizingAdapter({\n    profiles: () => profiles,\n    auth: INERT_AUTH,\n    // Resolve the shim's per-process shared secret as the OpenAI apiKey so\n    // pi-ai sends it as `Authorization: Bearer <shared-secret>`. The shim\n    // validates this before forwarding and resolves the real WorkBuddy token\n    // itself via the store, so the secret never reaches upstream.\n    resolveApiKey: async () => shim.token(),\n    ...resolveAttachments === undefined ? {} : { resolveAttachments },\n  }, effortCapable, resolveDefaultEffort)\n\n  return {\n    adapter,\n    invalidate: () => {\n      profiles = buildProfiles()\n    },\n  }\n}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "src/shim.ts",
      "content": "/**\n * Loopback OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The pi-ai provider points here; the\n * shim applies the WorkBuddy wire quirks (forced streaming, string\n * `tool_choice`, CLI-shaped headers) and forwards to the real upstream.\n * It binds 127.0.0.1 only and never serves another interface.\n *\n * 参考：corrinehu/dsh-workbuddy-connect（MIT，Copyright (c) 2026 Corrine Hu）\n *   — 入站加固的四重校验（Host 必须回环、Origin 必须回环、chat POST 必须\n *     JSON、bearer 必须匹配进程内随机 secret）、常量时间比对、\n *     随机端口绑定、body 上限、上游错误分类到 HTTP 状态码的映射，\n *     均由该项目设计并验证。\n * 改动：随机端口绑定加了「先确认客户端能用」一步（`listenOnClientReachablePort`）。\n *   绑定方式仍是 `listen(0)` + 仅回环，随机性不变；改的只是抽到 HTTP 客户端\n *   禁用端口（fetch 规范的 block-bad-port 清单）时重抽，而不是带着一个自己\n *   客户端连不上的端口启动。四重校验、常量时间比对、body 上限、\n *   错误分类均未改动。\n *\n * @module dsh-connect-workbuddy/shim\n */\n\nimport { randomBytes, timingSafeEqual } from 'node:crypto'\nimport { createServer, type IncomingMessage, type Server, type ServerResponse } from 'node:http'\nimport { Readable } from 'node:stream'\nimport type { WorkBuddyCredential, WorkBuddyCredentialStore } from './auth.ts'\nimport { workbuddyAccountId } from './auth.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyCatalog } from './catalog.ts'\nimport type { WorkBuddyRefusalReport } from './off-thinking.ts'\nimport { usageOfChunk } from './body-report.ts'\nimport { hostIsLoopback, originIsLoopback } from './loopback.ts'\nimport { DsmlStreamBuffer } from './dsml-recovery.ts'\nimport type { RecoveredToolCall, RecoveryGate } from './dsml-recovery.ts'\nimport { parseQueueAnswer, queueNoticeText } from './queue.ts'\nimport {\n  declaredTools,\n  prepareChatBody,\n  modelIdOfBody,\n  forceNeutralHead,\n  channelGateMemberOf,\n  isConversationIdGateBody,\n  isContextTooLongBody,\n  CONTEXT_OVERFLOW_MARKER,\n  isFirstMessageGateBody,\n  openingEvidenceOfBody,\n  perturbChannelFingerprints,\n  requestEvidenceOf,\n  rewriteSessionTitleModel,\n  WorkBuddyUpstreamClient,\n  type UpstreamErrorKind,\n  type WorkBuddyChatResult,\n} from './upstream.ts'\n\n/** Minimal logger surface the plugin context already provides. */\nexport interface ShimLogger {\n  warn(...args: unknown[]): void\n  error(...args: unknown[]): void\n}\n\n/** What the plugin needs from a running shim. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyShim {\n  /** Resolves once the listener is up; rejects if listening failed. */\n  ready: Promise<void>\n  /** The shim origin, e.g. `http://127.0.0.1:39271`; valid after ready. */\n  baseUrl(): string\n  /**\n   * The per-process shared secret the plugin's own client must carry as\n   * `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Lives only in memory; the adapter\n   * resolves this instead of the upstream access token, because the shim\n   * resolves the real credential itself via the store.\n   */\n  token(): string\n  /** Stop serving and destroy open connections. */\n  close(): Promise<void>\n}\n\n/**\n * Supplies the credential to retry a failed chat request with.\n *\n * Called with every account id already tried for THIS request, the one that just\n * failed LAST, and resolves to the next candidate — or `undefined` when the pool\n * is off, empty, or exhausted, in which case the original failure is reported\n * unchanged.\n *\n * The plugin owns the policy (which pool members count as usable, in what\n * order); the shim owns only the retry loop and the wire-level rule that a\n * retry may happen before anything has been written to the client.\n */\nexport type WorkBuddyFailoverAccount = (\n  triedAccountIds: readonly string[],\n) => Promise<WorkBuddyCredential | undefined>\n\n/**\n * Why the pool had no candidate, asked only after failover came up empty.\n *\n * A separate callback rather than a richer return type on\n * {@link WorkBuddyFailoverAccount}: the loop calls that one on the HOT PATH of\n * every retry, and a policy that had to explain its refusal on every call would\n * make the common case pay for the rare one. This runs at most once per failed\n * request, and only when the answer is already going to be an error.\n *\n * Returns a short Chinese clause naming the reason (e.g. \"池中其余账号都在冷却\"),\n * or undefined when the host has nothing to add — in which case the reply says\n * only that no other account was available.\n */\nexport type WorkBuddyFailoverShortfall = () => Promise<string | undefined>\n\n/** Constructor dependencies. */\nexport interface WorkBuddyShimOptions {\n  store: WorkBuddyCredentialStore\n  client: Pick<WorkBuddyUpstreamClient, 'chatStream'>\n  catalog: WorkBuddyCatalog\n  logger?: ShimLogger\n  /**\n   * When to perturb scanned messages with invisible characters.\n   *\n   * `off` keeps the ordinary fail-then-rescue ladder; `failure` (the default)\n   * remembers a successful rescue and starts later requests with it; `always`\n   * starts every request with the dense rescue. `always` is for users whose\n   * gateway has already made the plain attempt a guaranteed 11128 — it trades a\n   * little prompt byte-level purity for avoiding that doomed round trip.\n   */\n  channelPerturbation?: 'off' | 'failure' | 'always'\n  /**\n   * Move the harness's injected `user` blocks above the real prompt (issue #132).\n   *\n   * The WorkBuddy usage page records the LAST `user` message as the request\n   * text, and the harness appends its runtime context after the user's real\n   * prompt — so the usage column shows injected noise instead of what was typed.\n   * The reorder is order-only (every message and its text survive), and it only\n   * ever moves messages that MATCH the harness's own injected prefixes; the single\n   * textual edit is stripping the recorded prompt's own leading marker.\n   *\n   * Default false: it changes bytes the upstream counts, so it is opt-in.\n   */\n  keepPromptLast?: boolean\n  /**\n   * Extra literal prefixes (issue #133) treated like the harness's injected\n   * markers under `keepPromptLast`, for context blocks a companion plugin\n   * injects that the built-in list does not know about. Only read when\n   * `keepPromptLast` is on.\n   */\n  extraInjectedPrefixes?: readonly string[]\n  /**\n   * Move the host's session-title request onto the hidden lightweight model\n   * (issues #132/#133). Disabled by default; when enabled, a body recognized\n   * as the one-message title prompt is re-pointed at `model` (default\n   * `wb-lite`, measured 0 credits) instead of billing the main paid model.\n   * See `rewriteSessionTitleModel` for the fail-closed detection rules.\n   */\n  sessionTitle?: {\n    enabled?: boolean\n    model?: string\n  }\n  /**\n   * Pool failover for chat requests. Absent means no failover at all — the\n   * pre-pool behaviour, where the selected account serves and its failure is\n   * reported as-is.\n   */\n  failoverAccount?: WorkBuddyFailoverAccount\n  /**\n   * Why the pool had no usable candidate, asked after failover found none.\n   *\n   * The failure path uses this to answer the question the user is actually left\n   * with — \"why did nothing else work?\" — instead of only reporting that every\n   * attempt failed. Without it the pool's own reasoning is invisible at exactly\n   * the moment it matters, and \"换个模型\" reads as a guess rather than as the\n   * conclusion of a search the plugin already performed.\n   *\n   * Optional, and its absence is a supported state: a host that does not supply\n   * it produces the same reply as before this option existed.\n   */\n  failoverShortfall?: WorkBuddyFailoverShortfall\n  /**\n   * Told about every refused request the plugin can attribute to a MODEL, right\n   * before the failure is rendered (issue #83).\n   *\n   * Separate from the rendered reply because the caller that hit the refusal is\n   * not always the user: compaction reads the failure as a boolean and shows\n   * \"could not produce a useful summary\", so the one fact that explains it (this\n   * model refuses `off`) never reaches any surface. This callback is how the\n   * card learns it happened. Must not throw: a bookkeeping failure must never\n   * replace the upstream's own failure.\n   */\n  reportRefusal?: (refusal: WorkBuddyRefusalReport) => void\n  /**\n   * Called before the FIRST attempt of every chat request, so the plugin can\n   * point the store at the account the pool's ranking says should serve.\n   *\n   * It exists because \"who serves\" and \"who to retry with\" are the same decision\n   * made at two moments: the ranking picks the first account, and after a failure\n   * picks the next one. The shim resolves the credential itself right after this\n   * returns, so the ranking never has to produce a credential — which keeps\n   * token REFRESH in one place (`store.resolve()`) instead of duplicating it on\n   * a path that would silently send expired tokens.\n   *\n   * Absent means the store's own selection always serves.\n   */\n  prepareAccount?: () => Promise<void>\n  /**\n   * Called for every attempt that failed upstream, before the next one is tried.\n   *\n   * Exists so the plugin can RECORD the failure as a measurement. The ranking\n   * that picks the serving account reads the pool's STORED measurements, so\n   * without this an account that just answered 429 still reads as untested — and\n   * the next request picks it again and pays the same failed round trip before\n   * failing over.\n   *\n   * Fired without awaiting: a slow write must never delay the retry the user is\n   * waiting on, and the measurement only matters for LATER requests.\n   */\n  onAccountFailure?: (accountId: string, failure: {\n    status: number\n    kind: UpstreamErrorKind\n    message: string\n    /**\n     * The model that request asked for (issue #61).\n     *\n     * Carried because the failure is remembered against the ACCOUNT, and `6004`\n     * is an account × model condition: without this the record cannot say which\n     * model the measurement belongs to, so the card has to present a\n     * single-model result as account-level availability.\n     */\n    modelId?: string\n  }) => void\n  /**\n   * Called with the FINAL serialized body of every chat request, just before it\n   * goes upstream (issue #59).\n   *\n   * It exists so the plugin can record what was actually sent without the shim\n   * having to know where that record goes: the region belongs to whoever wires\n   * this up, and the report is a plugin concern. The body handed over is the\n   * post-`prepareChatBody` one, because THAT is what the upstream counts.\n   *\n   * Fired without awaiting: a request must never wait on a diagnostic. The\n   * callee must treat the string as read-only.\n   */\n  onRequestBody?: (bodyJson: string) => void\n  /**\n   * Called with the token counts the UPSTREAM reported for the request whose\n   * body was just handed to {@link onRequestBody} (issue #59).\n   *\n   * `onRequestBody` records what we SENT; this records what the gateway\n   * CHARGED. The issue cannot be settled with only one of them: a difference\n   * between two captures is ambiguous until both sides are known, because\n   * \"the gateway counted the same bytes differently\" and \"the two bodies were\n   * not the same\" look identical from the sent side alone.\n   *\n   * May fire zero times (the upstream reported no `usage`, or the stream was\n   * cut before its final frame) or more than once per body (a failover retry\n   * reaches a second upstream). The callee is expected to keep the FIRST\n   * real measurement, since that is the one the report's line belongs to.\n   *\n   * Fired without awaiting, and never allowed to fail the request.\n   */\n  onUpstreamUsage?: (usage: {\n    promptTokens?: number\n    completionTokens?: number\n    cachedTokens?: number\n  }) => void\n}\n\nconst REQUEST_BODY_LIMIT = 64 * 1024 * 1024\n\n/**\n * Pause between failover attempts, after one account's request failed.\n *\n * The upstream's rate limit (6004) fires on request volume — firing retries at\n * zero gap makes every candidate hit the same wall, and 4 accounts can all fail\n * in under a second. The batch test already waits `POOL_BATCH_GAP_MS` (400ms)\n * between accounts for exactly this reason; the failover loop used to wait\n * nothing. 2 seconds is longer than the batch's 400 because a failover retry is\n * a real request the user is waiting on, not a probe — it needs enough room for\n * the upstream's window to breathe, not just enough to avoid self-inflicted\n * rate-limiting.\n */\nconst FAILOVER_ACCOUNT_GAP_MS = 2_000\n\n/** Chat-completion POSTs must carry a JSON body type (simple-request CSRF drops here). */\nfunction isJsonContentType(req: IncomingMessage): boolean {\n  const type = req.headers['content-type']\n  return typeof type === 'string' && type.trim().toLowerCase().startsWith('application/json')\n}\n\n/** HTTP status each upstream failure class surfaces as. */\nconst KIND_STATUS: Readonly<Record<UpstreamErrorKind, number>> = {\n  hard_credit: 402,\n  soft_rate: 429,\n  // Same status as `soft_rate`, because that is what the upstream returned — the\n  // distinction lives in `code`/`type`, not in a relabelled status. See\n  // KIND_HOST_CODE for why the host must not read this as an account limit.\n  model_busy: 429,\n  session_dead: 401,\n  policy_reject: 403,\n  account_restricted: 403,\n  // A 400, because that is what the gateway answered and the request really is\n  // malformed from its point of view. See KIND_HOST_CODE for why the host must\n  // not read this as a retryable transport failure.\n  channel_reject: 400,\n  // A queue is a 429 in the sense the host understands: \"not now, try again\".\n  // The host's bounded in-place retry is exactly right for `6020` (the queue is\n  // moving), and for `6021`/`6022` the sentence says what to do instead.\n  model_queue: 429,\n  not_found: 502,\n  server: 502,\n  client: 400,\n}\n\n/**\n * The `code` spelling the HOST is expected to classify each upstream failure by.\n *\n * The host decides whether a failed request may be retried IN PLACE (5 attempts\n * with backoff) or must be handed to cross-provider failover by reading English\n * phrases out of `type + \" \" + code + \" \" + message` — see\n * `isQuotaExceededError` in `@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm`. Only `RATE_LIMIT`, `SERVER`,\n * `TIMEOUT`, `TRANSPORT` and `EMPTY_RESPONSE` are in its retry set, so the\n * spelling decides which of two very different things happens next.\n *\n * `soft_rate` is the one kind that spelling got wrong. The upstream's 429 says\n * \"usage exceeds the frequency limit, and it resets at 13:37\" — hours away, not\n * two seconds. Sent as `soft_rate` it reads as an ordinary rate limit, enters\n * the in-place retry set, and spends the whole retry budget on an endpoint that\n * cannot recover within it; by the time cross-provider failover is offered, the\n * turn has already failed. The upstream's own words are Chinese, so the\n * phrase-based classifier cannot see the distinction either — the label is the\n * only channel left to carry it.\n *\n * `quota_exceeded` is the minimal honest translation: it is the WORDS the host\n * recognises, and it matches what the upstream is actually saying. The HTTP\n * status stays 429 (that is what the upstream returned — relabelling it 402\n * would misreport the upstream), and the upstream's own message is still passed\n * through verbatim for a human to read.\n *\n * Every other kind already lands outside the retry set by its status alone\n * (`hard_credit` 402 → quota, `session_dead` 401 → auth, 502/400 → other), so\n * none of them is listed: a translation nobody needs is a lie waiting to drift.\n *\n * `policy_reject` is deliberately NOT listed either, for a sharper reason than\n * the others: the obvious candidate translation would be the upstream's own\n * numeric code (11140), but that is not a class the host knows, so it would take\n * the host's generic path and add nothing over `policy_reject`. It needs no\n * translation to stay outside the retry set (403), and it must NOT be translated\n * INTO the set — a content-policy refusal is deterministic, so retrying it in\n * place would spend the retry budget on a request the server will refuse again,\n * and cross-provider failover cannot help a policy decision either. The code the\n * host receives therefore names the class honestly.\n *\n * `model_busy` used to get the same `quota_exceeded` translation, for what\n * looked like the same reason, and that was WRONG in a way users reported\n * (issue #84). The reasoning above assumed `quota_exceeded` only influenced\n * retryability. It does more than that: `isQuotaExceededError` is matched\n * against `type + \" \" + code + \" \" + message`, the match yields host code\n * `QUOTA`, and the chat client hard-codes its copy for `QUOTA`/`ACCOUNT_QUOTA`\n * — substituting 「当前请求的额度已用尽」 and DISCARDING this plugin's message.\n * So a loaded model was shown to the user as an exhausted balance: an\n * actionable, transient condition rendered as a terminal, account-level one.\n *\n * Leaving `model_busy` untranslated is the correct spelling, and it was\n * verified end to end rather than reasoned about: driving pi-ai against a\n * server replaying this shim's real bodies, `code: 'model_busy'` classifies as\n * `RATE_LIMIT` — which (a) is outside the substitution list, so the plugin's\n * own honest sentence is what the user reads, and (b) IS in the host's\n * in-place retry set, so `dsh-llm-retry` performs a bounded retry with the\n * default 500 ms→10 s backoff instead of failing the turn on one 14003. That\n * retry is what makes \"the model is momentarily busy\" recoverable, which is\n * exactly what a 14003 is: measured against the same credential and second,\n * five consecutive sends gave 200/200/200/200/429.\n *\n * `soft_rate` keeps the translation, and the contrast is the whole point: its\n * reset is hours away, so the retry budget cannot produce an answer and must\n * not be spent. One word, two opposite temporal claims — so the spelling\n * follows the clock, not the status code both share.\n *\n * `channel_reject` is not listed, and the reason is the same rule applied to a\n * third clock: the gate's verdict is DETERMINISTIC for a given prompt, so an\n * in-place retry would spend the budget re-sending bytes it already refused, and\n * cross-provider failover cannot help either (every account sends that same\n * prompt). Its 400 already places it outside the host's retry set, so `type`\n * alone, untranslated, is the honest spelling.\n */\nconst KIND_HOST_CODE: Partial<Readonly<Record<UpstreamErrorKind, string>>> = {\n  soft_rate: 'quota_exceeded',\n}\n\n/**\n * The `code` to write for an upstream failure, given its classified kind.\n *\n * `type` deliberately stays the plugin's own kind: it is what a human reads in\n * a log or a bug report, and it keeps the two fields individually meaningful.\n * Only `code` carries the host-facing translation.\n */\nfunction hostErrorCode(kind: UpstreamErrorKind): string {\n  return KIND_HOST_CODE[kind] ?? kind\n}\n\nfunction writeJson(\n  res: ServerResponse,\n  status: number,\n  body: unknown,\n  headers?: Readonly<Record<string, string>>,\n): void {\n  const payload = JSON.stringify(body)\n  res.writeHead(status, {\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n    'Content-Length': Buffer.byteLength(payload),\n    ...headers,\n  })\n  res.end(payload)\n}\n\n/**\n * Write a JSON error the way an OpenAI-compatible client expects.\n *\n * `code` defaults to `type`, which is right for every error THIS shim raises on\n * its own (`unauthorized`, `not_found`, …): those are not upstream failures and\n * must not be disguised as one. The upstream failure path passes an explicit\n * `code` so the host classifies it correctly — see {@link KIND_HOST_CODE}.\n */\nfunction writeOpenAIError(\n  res: ServerResponse,\n  status: number,\n  kind: string,\n  message: string,\n  code?: string,\n  headers?: Readonly<Record<string, string>>,\n): void {\n  writeJson(res, status, { error: { message, type: kind, code: code ?? kind } }, headers)\n}\n\n/**\n * Tell every retrying client NOT to retry this failure (2026-10-22, at the\n * maintainer's request: the queue's automatic retry is removed).\n *\n * `x-should-retry` is a non-standard header that both clients in this stack\n * check BEFORE their status-based rules — the pinned OpenAI SDK's `shouldRetry`\n * and pi-ai's `isRetryableProviderError` each return false on \"false\".\n *\n * WHY THE QUEUE MUST NOT BE RETRIED. Without this header a 429 is retried by\n * construction: `@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm` maps status 429 to the `RATE_LIMIT` code\n * and `RATE_LIMIT` is in `DEFAULT_RETRYABLE_CODES`, so the HOST runs five\n * in-place attempts with 0.5s→8s backoff. Measured, that budget totals roughly\n * 15 seconds — against a queue the upstream reports in thousands. Every one of\n * those attempts is a real request spent to be told the same position again, and\n * none of them can clear the queue.\n *\n * So the queue is now reported ONCE, with the position intact, and the reader\n * decides whether to resend or switch models. That is exactly what\n * {@link queueNoticeText} tells them to do — the two changes are one decision: a\n * diagnostic that declares itself final must actually be final.\n */\nconst NO_RETRY_HEADERS: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = Object.freeze({\n  'x-should-retry': 'false',\n  // The same instruction in the standard spelling, for a client that prefers it\n  // over the vendor one. `0` means \"do not wait, do not retry\".\n  'retry-after': '0',\n})\n\n/**\n * An over-limit request body (issue #174).\n *\n * A named class rather than an `Error` with a magic message, because the two\n * callers must answer it with a DIFFERENT status: the protocol answer is 413,\n * and classifying it as an ordinary internal failure made it a 500. A class\n * survives renaming the message, which is the only reason this is not a\n * string comparison.\n */\nexport class RequestBodyTooLargeError extends Error {\n  constructor(readonly limitBytes: number) {\n    super(`request body too large (limit ${limitBytes} bytes)`)\n    this.name = 'RequestBodyTooLargeError'\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Read a request body with a size cap; over-limit bodies fail the request.\n *\n * AN OVER-LIMIT BODY IS STOPPED, NOT DESTROYED (issue #174). Calling\n * `req.destroy()` here tears down the SOCKET, so the 413 the caller is about to\n * write can never reach the client — it saw a reset connection instead of an\n * answer, which is strictly worse than the 500 this replaced. So the stream is\n * paused and its listeners are dropped: the request is no longer read, the\n * response still goes out, and Node closes the connection once the response\n * ends. Bytes already arriving are discarded with the socket, which is the\n * whole point of a cap (`content-length` is not trusted, so the cap has to be\n * enforced on arrival).\n */\nfunction readBody(req: IncomingMessage): Promise<Buffer> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const chunks: Buffer[] = []\n    let size = 0\n    req.on('data', (chunk: Buffer) => {\n      size += chunk.length\n      if (size > REQUEST_BODY_LIMIT) {\n        // Drop what was buffered before answering: holding a partial 64 MB body\n        // while writing a one-line error is pure waste.\n        chunks.length = 0\n        req.removeAllListeners('data')\n        req.removeAllListeners('end')\n        req.pause()\n        reject(new RequestBodyTooLargeError(REQUEST_BODY_LIMIT))\n        return\n      }\n      chunks.push(chunk)\n    })\n    req.on('end', () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks)))\n    req.on('error', reject)\n  })\n}\n\n/**\n * Rewrites the upstream SSE stream, converting markup the model wrote into the\n * text back into a real `delta.tool_calls` — and, when a whole turn produced\n * nothing but markup, holding the bytes long enough to retry ONCE.\n *\n * ============================================================================\n * Why this class exists rather than a `.pipe()`\n * ============================================================================\n *\n * Recovering a call means inspecting `delta.content` before the client sees it,\n * which the old `body.pipe(res)` could not do. The parsing rules live in\n * `src/dsml-recovery.ts`; everything here is about the STREAM's obligations:\n *\n *   1. FRAME ORDER IS NEVER CHANGED. Frames are forwarded in arrival order.\n *      The only frame this class invents is the trailing one that carries a\n *      block still unfinished at end-of-stream.\n *\n *   2. HOLDS ARE TEMPORARY AND PAY FOR THEMSELVES. Nothing is held unless the\n *      answer so far consists only of markup: the moment real prose or a real\n *      call appears, everything held is flushed in order and the stream returns\n *      to plain pass-through. An ordinary answer therefore pays no latency at\n *      all, and no byte is ever discarded while a retry is still possible.\n *\n *   3. A RETRY MUST NOT SPLICE TWO ANSWERS TOGETHER. That constraint is why the\n *      hold exists: because no content frame has been written yet, the caller\n *      can discard this attempt and re-send the same body. The existing\n *      failover loop makes the same promise for failures BEFORE the stream\n *      starts (see its comment in `chatCompletions`); this extends it to the\n *      one case that can only be recognised after the fact.\n *\n *   4. NOTHING IS INVENTED WHEN THE UPSTREAM ALREADY SPEAKS STRUCTURED CALLS.\n *      Once a native `delta.tool_calls` appears, recovery switches off for the\n *      rest of the response — two live call channels for one answer is worse\n *      than either one alone.\n *\n *   5. EVERY FRAME LEAVES SELF-CONTAINED. An SSE event ends at a blank line,\n *      and a frame written without its terminator merges into the NEXT event\n *      the client decodes — whose `data:` lines are then joined and parsed as\n *      one document (issue #53). Every write therefore goes through\n *      {@link emitFrame}, which completes the terminator; `push()` cuts frames\n *      that already carry it, and the one source of unterminated bytes — the\n *      half-frame left in `carry` when the upstream dies — is normalized in\n *      {@link consume}.\n */\nclass RecoveryStream {\n  private carry = ''\n  private readonly decoder = new TextDecoder('utf-8')\n  /** Frames received after the hold began, in arrival order. */\n  private held: Array<{ frame: string; final: boolean }> = []\n  /**\n   * The hold window, as two separate facts.\n   *\n   * `windowOpened` becomes true at the FIRST content-bearing frame and stays\n   * true: before it, frames carry no answer (a role delta, a keep-alive) and go\n   * straight out; from it on, they are held until the answer is known to be\n   * real. `windowClosed` means \"an answer was delivered\", after which every\n   * frame passes through again — for the rest of the response, because a turn\n   * that has already produced prose can no longer be replaced by a retry.\n   */\n  private windowOpened = false\n  private windowClosed = false\n  private wroteContent = false\n  private sawResidue = false\n  private sawDone = false\n  /**\n   * Set once the upstream speaks structured calls.\n   *\n   * Sticky for the whole response on purpose: recovering markup into a second\n   * call channel after the upstream already produced a real one would hand the\n   * client two answers to the same question.\n   */\n  private nativeCallsSeen = false\n\n  private readonly buffer: DsmlStreamBuffer | undefined\n\n  /**\n   * A notice to place at the head of the answer, once, before any prose.\n   *\n   * Set when the request reached this stream only after the pool failed over, so\n   * the user learns WHY the answer came from a different account. Without it a\n   * failover is invisible: the reply looks like it came from the account they\n   * picked, and the only trace is a `logger.warn` they never see. The notice is\n   * emitted ahead of the model's own text so it cannot be mistaken for part of\n   * the answer, and only once — a retry that never delivers text must not leave\n   * a stray line behind.\n   */\n  private notice: string | undefined\n\n  constructor(\n    private readonly res: ServerResponse,\n    gate: RecoveryGate | undefined,\n    private readonly logger: ShimLogger | undefined,\n    notice?: string,\n    /** Called once, with the upstream's token counts, when the stream reports them. */\n    private readonly onUsage?: (usage: {\n      promptTokens?: number\n      completionTokens?: number\n      cachedTokens?: number\n    }) => void,\n  ) {\n    this.buffer = gate === undefined ? undefined : new DsmlStreamBuffer(gate)\n    this.notice = notice\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Emit the pending failover notice, if any, and forget it.\n   *\n   * Called immediately before the first content reaches the client. Placed\n   * here rather than in the constructor because a stream whose write fails, or\n   * whose attempt is discarded in favour of a retry, must not have already told\n   * the user about a switch it never completed.\n   */\n  private flushNotice(): void {\n    const notice = this.notice\n    if (notice === undefined) return\n    this.notice = undefined\n    if (!this.res.writable) return\n    const chunk: Record<string, unknown> = { choices: [{ index: 0, delta: {}, finish_reason: '' }] }\n    const choiceRecord: Record<string, unknown> = { index: 0, delta: {}, finish_reason: '' }\n    // Written straight to the socket, deliberately bypassing the hold window:\n    // the notice is ours, not the upstream's, so it is not something a retry\n    // could need to replace — and putting it through the window would let a\n    // discarded attempt take it away again after the user had seen it.\n    this.windowOpened = true\n    this.emitFrame(contentFrame(chunk, choiceRecord, notice))\n  }\n\n  /** True when a call or real prose has reached the client. */\n  get delivered(): boolean {\n    return this.wroteContent\n  }\n\n  /** True when the held bytes contain markup the gates refused. */\n  get residueSeen(): boolean {\n    return this.sawResidue\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The single exit every byte of a FRAME passes through.\n   *\n   * SSE dispatches one event per blank-line-terminated run of `data:` lines.\n   * A frame written WITHOUT its terminator leaves the client's decoder\n   * waiting for a blank line that never arrives, so the NEXT write is\n   * appended to the SAME event — and a client that joins the several `data:`\n   * lines of one event with `\\n` and parses the result as one JSON document\n   * (openai's `SSEDecoder` does exactly that) then reads two payloads as one\n   * and fails. Issue #53 is that failure: the upstream died between a `data:`\n   * line and its blank line, `consume()` forwarded the half-frame verbatim,\n   * and `finish()` glued its synthetic `[DONE]` onto it, producing\n   * `data: {...}\\ndata: [DONE]\\n\\n` — parsed as one event whose data is\n   * `\"{...}\\n[DONE]\"`, which throws \"Unexpected non-whitespace character\n   * after JSON at position N\". The whole turn is then classified as a\n   * non-retryable error, so the user simply sees the answer stop.\n   *\n   * Completing the terminator here fixes both halves at once: the truncated\n   * frame becomes a self-contained event, and the `[DONE]` that follows can no\n   * longer be joined to it. It is idempotent for the frames `push()` cuts —\n   * those always carry the terminator already, so they pass through\n   * byte-for-byte, which keeps guarantee 1 (frame order is never changed)\n   * intact.\n   *\n   * Note that a missing NEWLINE is also completed: `data: {…}` cut mid-line\n   * needs the full `\\n\\n`, not a single `\\n`, to close the event. Such a frame\n   * is upstream-corrupted JSON that cannot be repaired here; closing it at\n   * least keeps the corruption confined to its own event instead of taking\n   * the terminator down with it.\n   */\n  private emitFrame(frame: string): void {\n    if (frame.endsWith('\\n\\n')) {\n      this.res.write(frame)\n      return\n    }\n    this.res.write(frame.endsWith('\\n') ? `${frame}\\n` : `${frame}\\n\\n`)\n  }\n\n  /** Record that text the gates refused was seen (markup residue, not prose). */\n  private noteResidue(seen: boolean): void {\n    this.sawResidue ||= seen\n  }\n\n  /** Drain one upstream body into the client. */\n  async consume(body: ReadableStream<Uint8Array> | null): Promise<void> {\n    if (body === null) return\n    const source = Readable.fromWeb(body as Parameters<typeof Readable.fromWeb>[0])\n    try {\n      for await (const chunk of source) {\n        this.push(this.decoder.decode(chunk as Buffer, { stream: true }))\n      }\n    } catch (error: unknown) {\n      // The old pass-through logged the same way and let the response end with\n      // a synthesized terminator; that behaviour is preserved by `finish()`.\n      this.logger?.warn('dsh-connect-workbuddy: upstream stream failed mid-flight', error)\n    }\n    const tail = this.carry\n    this.carry = ''\n    if (tail !== '') {\n      // `push()` only cuts a frame at an EMPTY line, so whatever is left in\n      // `carry` when the upstream ends is by definition a frame that never\n      // received its terminator — the upstream died between a `data:` line and\n      // the blank line that would have closed it. Forwarding it verbatim leaves\n      // the client's SSE decoder inside that event, so the `[DONE]` written by\n      // `finish()` becomes a SECOND `data:` line of the SAME event and the two\n      // payloads are joined and parsed as one JSON document — issue #53's\n      // \"Unexpected non-whitespace character after JSON at position N\".\n      //\n      // Closing the frame here keeps it a self-contained event: the last real\n      // chunk of the answer is delivered instead of being lost (without a\n      // terminator the client never dispatches the event at all and silently\n      // drops that content), and the synthetic terminator that follows can no\n      // longer be glued onto it. `emitFrame` completes the terminator for every\n      // write, so this normalization is the tail's share of that guarantee.\n      const lineTerminated = tail.endsWith('\\n')\n      this.handleFrame(lineTerminated ? `${tail}\\n` : `${tail}\\n\\n`)\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Close the response: emit the block still unfinished at end-of-stream, then\n   * everything held, then the terminator.\n   */\n  finish(): void {\n    const flushed = this.buffer?.flush()\n    if (flushed !== undefined && flushed.text !== '') {\n      this.noteResidue(!flushed.prose)\n      this.emitContent(flushed.text)\n    }\n    this.closeWindow()\n    if (!this.sawDone && this.res.writable) this.emitFrame('data: [DONE]\\n\\n')\n    if (this.res.writable) this.res.end()\n  }\n\n  /** Drop held bytes without writing them (used when a retry replaced them). */\n  discard(): void {\n    this.held = []\n    this.windowClosed = true\n  }\n\n  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  /** Split incoming bytes into whole SSE frames, handling chunk boundaries. */\n  private push(text: string): void {\n    this.carry += text\n    let start = 0\n    for (;;) {\n      const newline = this.carry.indexOf('\\n', start)\n      if (newline === -1) break\n      const line = this.carry.slice(start, newline)\n      if (line.trim() === '') {\n        const frame = this.carry.slice(0, newline + 1)\n        this.carry = this.carry.slice(newline + 1)\n        start = 0\n        this.handleFrame(frame)\n        continue\n      }\n      start = newline + 1\n    }\n  }\n\n  /** Whether a usage measurement has already been reported for this stream. */\n  private usageReported = false\n\n  /**\n   * Read the upstream's token counts out of one parsed chunk and report them.\n   *\n   * FIRES AT MOST ONCE per stream, and only when there is a real number to\n   * report. Two reasons, both load-bearing